20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":526,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>has been blowing pretty fresh all night from the SE &amp; SSE. Got up at 8 o'clock am. The ship abreast of <span class=highlight_yellow>Hunt</span><span class=highlight_yellow>c</span><span class=highlight_yellow>li</span><span class=highlight_yellow>f</span><span class=highlight_yellow>f</span> Foot. The wind the same but moderate &amp; fine weather. Anne very unwell, but more frightened not having been at sea before. Towards evening wind very calm again. Turned in at 10 o'clock.  The ship in <span class=highlight_yellow>a very</span> little wind.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":508,"lock":null}
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/2/509
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":509,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning blowing rather fresh from NW by W. Walked with Tom Smith down to Low Town &amp; afterwards to Alec Sadlers at Whitle Point. Dined at home &amp; after tea again walked to Whitle Point but the sloop had not come down.</p>","pageId":732,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":491,"lock":null}
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/2/509
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":509,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning blowing rather fresh from NW by W. Walked with Tom Smith down to Low Town &amp; afterwards to Alec Sadlers at Whitle Point. Dined at home &amp; after tea again walked to Whitle Point but the sloop had not come down.</p>","pageId":732,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":491,"lock":null}
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/20/527
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":527,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning. Wind NE. Turned out a 8 o'clock am. The ship off Robin Hoods but the ebb tide &amp; heavy SE swell setting against us drove us <span class=highlight_yellow>back</span> to Whitby. In the afternoon with the <span class=highlight_yellow>fload</span> a better breeze spring up &amp; set us ahead again &amp; 10 o'clock pm bed time, we were off Scarborough. A fine moonlit night.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":509,"lock":null}
20:13:19.059 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/20/527
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":527,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning. Wind NE. Turned out a 8 o'clock am. The ship off Robin Hoods but the ebb tide &amp; heavy SE swell setting against us drove us <span class=highlight_yellow>back</span> to Whitby. In the afternoon with the <span class=highlight_yellow>fload</span> a better breeze spring up &amp; set us ahead again &amp; 10 o'clock pm bed time, we were off Scarborough. A fine moonlit night.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":509,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/21/528
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":528,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at 8 o'clock am been a fresh breeze all night from NE. Got round the Head at 5 o'clock am. A fine morning but rather dull. Had a good sun. About 9 o'clock Cromer. At 10 o'clock turned in being <span class=highlight_yellow>marhasborough</span>. Ann Stokes 1<sup>st</sup> <span class=highlight_yellow>time</span>.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":510,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/21/528
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":528,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at 8 o'clock am been a fresh breeze all night from NE. Got round the Head at 5 o'clock am. A fine morning but rather dull. Had a good sun. About 9 o'clock Cromer. At 10 o'clock turned in being <span class=highlight_yellow>marhasborough</span>. Ann Stokes 1<sup>st</sup> <span class=highlight_yellow>time</span>.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":510,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/22/529
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":529,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock am. Very thick weather, but a fine breeze at NE, then appeared to be off Yarmouth behind the sands. We ran until about 1 o'clock pm when we took in studding sails &amp; stood in for the land, which we made a little to the northward of Aldeburgh. The weather clearer from thence. We took our departure to run down <span class=highlight_yellow>raren</span> . About 4 o'clock it turned out a beautiful clear day. At 6 o'clock pm abreast of Orford. At 10 pm being dark, brought up abreast of the Hook of the middle. At a little after 12 o'clock went to bed. A fine moonlit night. With Ann Stokes again!!</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":511,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/22/529
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":529,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 8 o'clock am. Very thick weather, but a fine breeze at NE, then appeared to be off Yarmouth behind the sands. We ran until about 1 o'clock pm when we took in studding sails &amp; stood in for the land, which we made a little to the northward of Aldeburgh. The weather clearer from thence. We took our departure to run down <span class=highlight_yellow>raren</span> . About 4 o'clock it turned out a beautiful clear day. At 6 o'clock pm abreast of Orford. At 10 pm being dark, brought up abreast of the Hook of the middle. At a little after 12 o'clock went to bed. A fine moonlit night. With Ann Stokes again!!</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":511,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/23/530
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":530,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>At 3 o'clock <font color=#BFBFBF>[in]</font> the morning they got the ship under way, with a light breeze from the NE. At 7 o'clock I got up &amp; went upon deck, past through a large fleet of light ships. At 8 o'clock got the flood with a little more wind. Again passed a great number of light ships. Between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock brought up a little blow Gravesend. The crew went to bed early. Ann &amp; I walked the deck until after 10 o'clock. Sat up until 12 o'clock. Went to bed to Ann</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":512,"lock":null}
20:13:19.060 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/23/530
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":530,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>At 3 o'clock <font color=#BFBFBF>[in]</font> the morning they got the ship under way, with a light breeze from the NE. At 7 o'clock I got up &amp; went upon deck, past through a large fleet of light ships. At 8 o'clock got the flood with a little more wind. Again passed a great number of light ships. Between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock brought up a little blow Gravesend. The crew went to bed early. Ann &amp; I walked the deck until after 10 o'clock. Sat up until 12 o'clock. Went to bed to Ann</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":512,"lock":null}
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/24/531
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":531,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up about 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Wind unfortunately changed to W so that at the early morning tide, we could not move, There also being very little of it. After breakfast dressed myself &amp; landed at Gravesend about 11 o'clock, waited until <span class=highlight_yellow>about</span> 1 o'clock before I could get a coach. Got to town at 4 o'clock pm. Left the ships papers at the office &amp; got to Nottingham Place at &frac12; past 5 o'clock. Found them all well &amp; glad to see me. Bob Marsh &amp; his father dined with us. Heavy rain in the afternoon.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":513,"lock":null}
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/24/531
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":531,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up about 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Wind unfortunately changed to W so that at the early morning tide, we could not move, There also being very little of it. After breakfast dressed myself &amp; landed at Gravesend about 11 o'clock, waited until <span class=highlight_yellow>about</span> 1 o'clock before I could get a coach. Got to town at 4 o'clock pm. Left the ships papers at the office &amp; got to Nottingham Place at &frac12; past 5 o'clock. Found them all well &amp; glad to see me. Bob Marsh &amp; his father dined with us. Heavy rain in the afternoon.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":513,"lock":null}
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/25/532
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":532,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but blowing hard from the N by E. William &amp; I walked into the city. Called upon Bob Marsh only a short time at the office, when Stephenson brought Ann up. I walked with her to Grays Inn, showed her St Pants, went round by Oxford &amp; Regents street &amp; Ann feet being very sore we after buying a pair of easy shoes, took a coach &amp; drove to  new inn. Dined with Bob Marsh &amp; afterwards we all went to the pit at Covent garden Theatre. Ann much astonished at the size &amp; splendour of the houses. We saw Romeo &amp; Juliet performed by C Kenneth &amp; Mrs Smithson. Then afterwards the Devils Elixir, not worth seeing. Ann &amp; I slept at the Fountain in Catherine Street</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":514,"lock":null}
20:13:19.061 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/25/532
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":532,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but blowing hard from the N by E. William &amp; I walked into the city. Called upon Bob Marsh only a short time at the office, when Stephenson brought Ann up. I walked with her to Grays Inn, showed her St Pants, went round by Oxford &amp; Regents street &amp; Ann feet being very sore we after buying a pair of easy shoes, took a coach &amp; drove to  new inn. Dined with Bob Marsh &amp; afterwards we all went to the pit at Covent garden Theatre. Ann much astonished at the size &amp; splendour of the houses. We saw Romeo &amp; Juliet performed by C Kenneth &amp; Mrs Smithson. Then afterwards the Devils Elixir, not worth seeing. Ann &amp; I slept at the Fountain in Catherine Street</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":514,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/26/533
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":533,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful day. Wind still fresh from the N by E. We got breakfast at 11 o'clock &amp; afterwards walked into the Green park round by the Kings Place by Pall Mall to Somerset House &amp; saw the exhibition. Ann much pleased with her mornings <span class=highlight_yellow>amusements</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>took</span> &amp;  left in Grays Inn. Walked into the city. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>Changes</span>, did nothing with the ship &amp; walked to Nottingham Place to dinner. The Hope could not be sold today.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":515,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/26/533
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":533,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful day. Wind still fresh from the N by E. We got breakfast at 11 o'clock &amp; afterwards walked into the Green park round by the Kings Place by Pall Mall to Somerset House &amp; saw the exhibition. Ann much pleased with her mornings <span class=highlight_yellow>amusements</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>took</span> &amp;  left in Grays Inn. Walked into the city. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>Changes</span>, did nothing with the ship &amp; walked to Nottingham Place to dinner. The Hope could not be sold today.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":515,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/27/534
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":534,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":27,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another beautiful morning. Wind blowing fresh at N by E. Called upon Mr ward. Walked into the city. On my way saw Ann. Attended the coal market with much difficulty they sold <span class=highlight_yellow>58/2</span> of the Hope's cargo. Walked out of the city. Saw Ann again. Called upon Richmond found him looking well, though still lame. Returned by Regents <span class=highlight_yellow>He</span> to Mr Lee to dinner. Bob marsh with us.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":516,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/27/534
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":534,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":27,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another beautiful morning. Wind blowing fresh at N by E. Called upon Mr ward. Walked into the city. On my way saw Ann. Attended the coal market with much difficulty they sold <span class=highlight_yellow>58/2</span> of the Hope's cargo. Walked out of the city. Saw Ann again. Called upon Richmond found him looking well, though still lame. Returned by Regents <span class=highlight_yellow>He</span> to Mr Lee to dinner. Bob marsh with us.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":516,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/28/535
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":535,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":28,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance of fine weather. Wind NE. Wrote to Mr Vernon to send <span class=highlight_yellow>Inspector</span> Galbraith to London. Walked into the city. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>changers</span>. Did nothing. Dined with Richmond.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":517,"lock":null}
20:13:19.062 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/28/535
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":535,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":28,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance of fine weather. Wind NE. Wrote to Mr Vernon to send <span class=highlight_yellow>Inspector</span> Galbraith to London. Walked into the city. Attended <span class=highlight_yellow>changers</span>. Did nothing. Dined with Richmond.</p>","pageId":735,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":517,"lock":null}
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/29/536
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":536,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":29,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning but fine weather. Wind still NE &amp; ENE. Mr Lee drove me into the city. Attended the local exchange. Sold 63 more of the Hope's coals. Went to the Royal Ex. Could not fix. Walked out of the city. Dined at Mr Lees. Mr &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; F Barclay there.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":518,"lock":null}
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/29/536
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":536,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":29,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning but fine weather. Wind still NE &amp; ENE. Mr Lee drove me into the city. Attended the local exchange. Sold 63 more of the Hope's coals. Went to the Royal Ex. Could not fix. Walked out of the city. Dined at Mr Lees. Mr &amp; Bob Marsh &amp; F Barclay there.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":518,"lock":null}
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/3/510
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":510,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":3,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine mild morning after a few showers of rain during the night. Wind W by S. Tom Smith, Emma &amp; I went to church &amp; after <font color=#BFBFBF>[the] </font>service, Tom &amp; I walked up through the country to Benton, dined &amp; stayed all night. A very windy day &amp; unpleasant walking.</p>","pageId":732,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":492,"lock":null}
20:13:19.063 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/3/510
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":510,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":3,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine mild morning after a few showers of rain during the night. Wind W by S. Tom Smith, Emma &amp; I went to church &amp; after <font color=#BFBFBF>[the] </font>service, Tom &amp; I walked up through the country to Benton, dined &amp; stayed all night. A very windy day &amp; unpleasant walking.</p>","pageId":732,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":492,"lock":null}
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/30/537
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":537,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another cloudy morning. Wind NE. Walked into the city. Called upon Bob Marsh. He &amp; his father walked with me down to the Hope. Saw the St Catherine Docks in our way &amp; the most perfect &amp; comfortable perhaps of thing of the sort all the warehouses &amp; upon so good a place. Had lunch on board &amp; I afterwards attended changes but did nothing for the ship. Walked out of the city with Inspector Chapman &amp; dined with him. His brothers the captain, Thomas, Ben, Mr Graham &amp; a Mr Day there.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":519,"lock":null}
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/30/537
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":537,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":30,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another cloudy morning. Wind NE. Walked into the city. Called upon Bob Marsh. He &amp; his father walked with me down to the Hope. Saw the St Catherine Docks in our way &amp; the most perfect &amp; comfortable perhaps of thing of the sort all the warehouses &amp; upon so good a place. Had lunch on board &amp; I afterwards attended changes but did nothing for the ship. Walked out of the city with Inspector Chapman &amp; dined with him. His brothers the captain, Thomas, Ben, Mr Graham &amp; a Mr Day there.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":519,"lock":null}
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/31/538
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":538,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull cloudy morning. Wind about N. Went with the Miss Lees to the chapel in Vern Street. Afterwards called upon Mr &amp; Mrs Lumley in Gower Street. Mr Lee very unwell, consumption, going to leave this <span class=highlight_yellow>home</span>. Called upon Mr Linskill. Went to see Mary &amp; Emma in Sloan Street &amp; then to the park. Dined with Mr ward &amp; spent a pleasant day.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":520,"lock":null}
20:13:19.064 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/31/538
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":538,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":31,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull cloudy morning. Wind about N. Went with the Miss Lees to the chapel in Vern Street. Afterwards called upon Mr &amp; Mrs Lumley in Gower Street. Mr Lee very unwell, consumption, going to leave this <span class=highlight_yellow>home</span>. Called upon Mr Linskill. Went to see Mary &amp; Emma in Sloan Street &amp; then to the park. Dined with Mr ward &amp; spent a pleasant day.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":520,"lock":null}
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/4/511
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":511,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. But still blowing hard from the NW. Amused ourselves for a short time in the plantations with the beagles. Mrs Dixon took us into Newcastle in the carriage. Tom Smith transacted his business there &amp; we afterwards walked down to St Anthon's to see a sloop he had purchased for his colliery. Returned to Shields by steam to dinner.</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":493,"lock":null}
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/4/511
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":511,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":4,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. But still blowing hard from the NW. Amused ourselves for a short time in the plantations with the beagles. Mrs Dixon took us into Newcastle in the carriage. Tom Smith transacted his business there &amp; we afterwards walked down to St Anthon's to see a sloop he had purchased for his colliery. Returned to Shields by steam to dinner.</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":493,"lock":null}
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/5/512
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":512,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine spring morning with showers. Wind SSW. Several light ships arrived. Tom &amp; Mrs Smith left us this morning for Benton. Dined at home. Accepted a bill at 3 <span class=highlight_yellow>months</span> dated 16 April for polices &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Insurance</span> 1 &amp; 2 coal voyages in general Premium Club &pound;13.7.10. With Bell Jervis in the evening</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":494,"lock":null}
20:13:19.065 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/5/512
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":512,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine spring morning with showers. Wind SSW. Several light ships arrived. Tom &amp; Mrs Smith left us this morning for Benton. Dined at home. Accepted a bill at 3 <span class=highlight_yellow>months</span> dated 16 April for polices &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Insurance</span> 1 &amp; 2 coal voyages in general Premium Club &pound;13.7.10. With Bell Jervis in the evening</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":494,"lock":null}
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/6/513
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":513,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning. Wind SW. Called upon &amp; spent an hour with Fergusson the artist. Dined at home. Afterwards walked to Tynemouth with Fergusson to sketch the ruins. In the evening at <span class=highlight_yellow>Davies</span> Rooms at the Castle</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":495,"lock":null}
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/6/513
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":513,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A beautiful morning. Wind SW. Called upon &amp; spent an hour with Fergusson the artist. Dined at home. Afterwards walked to Tynemouth with Fergusson to sketch the ruins. In the evening at <span class=highlight_yellow>Davies</span> Rooms at the Castle</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":495,"lock":null}
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/7/514
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":514,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant spring morning. Wind NNW. Changed during the day to NE &amp; again at night to W. Took a walk round by Cullercoat Fields &amp; Tynemouth. A beautiful summers day. Dined with Jackson 80<sup>th</sup> Regiment at the Castle. H Dale, Dacres, RA. Newton &amp; a Lieutenant Hamilton Brown there. (with 1<sup>st</sup> term)</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":496,"lock":null}
20:13:19.066 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/7/514
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":514,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant spring morning. Wind NNW. Changed during the day to NE &amp; again at night to W. Took a walk round by Cullercoat Fields &amp; Tynemouth. A beautiful summers day. Dined with Jackson 80<sup>th</sup> Regiment at the Castle. H Dale, Dacres, RA. Newton &amp; a Lieutenant Hamilton Brown there. (with 1<sup>st</sup> term)</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":496,"lock":null}
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/8/515
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":515,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A wet morning . A fine mild rain. Wind W by N. Called upon Fergusson Dined at home. The Hope arrived this evening after a fine passage. Left Gravesend on Wednesday morning.</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":497,"lock":null}
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/8/515
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":515,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A wet morning . A fine mild rain. Wind W by N. Called upon Fergusson Dined at home. The Hope arrived this evening after a fine passage. Left Gravesend on Wednesday morning.</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":497,"lock":null}
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/9/516
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":516,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine spring morning. Some showers during the night. Warmer growing weather at last. Wind W by N. Mr Fergusson &amp; I went to Newcastle by steam on the quay, but could do nothing for the Hope. Called upon Inspector Hodgson &amp; dined with him at his brothers. Returned to Shields in the evening. With Bell Jarvis, remained with her until 4 o'clock in the morning. Received from Geo Dunn &amp; sons &pound;27 being the difference between the freight made &amp; that upheld <span class=highlight_yellow>=rig</span>: 8/6</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":498,"lock":null}
20:13:19.067 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/5/9/516
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":516,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine spring morning. Some showers during the night. Warmer growing weather at last. Wind W by N. Mr Fergusson &amp; I went to Newcastle by steam on the quay, but could do nothing for the Hope. Called upon Inspector Hodgson &amp; dined with him at his brothers. Returned to Shields in the evening. With Bell Jarvis, remained with her until 4 o'clock in the morning. Received from Geo Dunn &amp; sons &pound;27 being the difference between the freight made &amp; that upheld <span class=highlight_yellow>=rig</span>: 8/6</p>","pageId":733,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":498,"lock":null}
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/1/539
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":539,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very dull morning &amp; light rain. Wind NW. Walked into the city. At the coal exchange the remainder of the Hope sold. Attended the Royal Exchange but could not fix the Hope. Walked to Mr Lees to dinner. Bob Marsh dined with us.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":521,"lock":null}
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/1/539
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":539,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very dull morning &amp; light rain. Wind NW. Walked into the city. At the coal exchange the remainder of the Hope sold. Attended the Royal Exchange but could not fix the Hope. Walked to Mr Lees to dinner. Bob Marsh dined with us.</p>","pageId":736,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":521,"lock":null}
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/10/553
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":553,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. Turned out between 7 &amp; * o'clock am &amp; breakfasted. The ship under way with a foul wind at NNE. Bob Marsh, John G<font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>lb</font><font color=#BFBFBF>raith</font> &amp; I got a waterman &amp; returned to London to see William again &amp; get some of our cloths. Gave WBS a my note on <span class=highlight_yellow>detailed</span> for &pound;300 because <span class=highlight_yellow>int</span> I unfortunately being considerably in his debt. We all dined in Water lane at 3 o'clock pm &amp; at 4 o'clock embarked on board to the Hope, lying at Gallions. Had tea. Employed ourselves in the evening stowing all our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>&amp;</span> away &amp; getting things into something like shipshape. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> a couple of glasses of gin &amp; water &amp; about 11 o'clock turned in, feeling my cold rather worse. With a <span class=highlight_yellow>cough</span> at my chest. Wrote today to the Sadler declining to join him in the establishment of a paper manufactury at Shields.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":531,"lock":null}
20:13:19.068 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/10/553
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":553,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. Turned out between 7 &amp; * o'clock am &amp; breakfasted. The ship under way with a foul wind at NNE. Bob Marsh, John G<font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>lb</font><font color=#BFBFBF>raith</font> &amp; I got a waterman &amp; returned to London to see William again &amp; get some of our cloths. Gave WBS a my note on <span class=highlight_yellow>detailed</span> for &pound;300 because <span class=highlight_yellow>int</span> I unfortunately being considerably in his debt. We all dined in Water lane at 3 o'clock pm &amp; at 4 o'clock embarked on board to the Hope, lying at Gallions. Had tea. Employed ourselves in the evening stowing all our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>&amp;</span> away &amp; getting things into something like shipshape. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> a couple of glasses of gin &amp; water &amp; about 11 o'clock turned in, feeling my cold rather worse. With a <span class=highlight_yellow>cough</span> at my chest. Wrote today to the Sadler declining to join him in the establishment of a paper manufactury at Shields.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":531,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/11/554
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":554,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":11,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out a little after 7 o'clock am. Thick weather &amp; wind very variable between E &amp; NNW. At &frac12; past 9 o'clock got under way &amp; worked down with the tide. At &frac12; past 3 o'clock being low water, we brought up at Grays &amp; had our dinner. Several very fine sailing boats past us, being a race for a cup, in the following order as they returned after going round the boat at <span class=highlight_yellow>south</span> fleet, the <span class=highlight_yellow>Einnlation</span> , Rob Bery, Yela, Lady Luisa &amp; Royal Eagle.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob &amp; I <span class=highlight_yellow>discerfsed</span> a bottle of Madiera. At 7 o'clock had tea. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock being high water we again got under way &amp; worked down to Gravesend where we brought up for the night at 10 o'clock, smoked two pipes, finished the like number of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; at 11 o'clock went to roost. Wind very variable during the day between SE and NNW.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":532,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/11/554
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":554,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":11,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out a little after 7 o'clock am. Thick weather &amp; wind very variable between E &amp; NNW. At &frac12; past 9 o'clock got under way &amp; worked down with the tide. At &frac12; past 3 o'clock being low water, we brought up at Grays &amp; had our dinner. Several very fine sailing boats past us, being a race for a cup, in the following order as they returned after going round the boat at <span class=highlight_yellow>south</span> fleet, the <span class=highlight_yellow>Einnlation</span> , Rob Bery, Yela, Lady Luisa &amp; Royal Eagle.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob &amp; I <span class=highlight_yellow>discerfsed</span> a bottle of Madiera. At 7 o'clock had tea. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock being high water we again got under way &amp; worked down to Gravesend where we brought up for the night at 10 o'clock, smoked two pipes, finished the like number of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; at 11 o'clock went to roost. Wind very variable during the day between SE and NNW.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":532,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/12/555
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":555,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 7 o'clock. Every appearance of being a very after a thick foggy night. Wind about East. After breakfast Bob Marsh &amp; I went on shore to endeavour to get each a straw hat, but did not succeed. Returned to the ship again before 10 o'clock. During our absence they had got her under way. At low water having had a fresh breeze from the E to ESE. We brought up abreast of Canvey Island, off Leigh at &frac12; past 2 o'clock. Had our dinner which <span class=highlight_yellow>was</span> devoured with a pretty considerable appetite.  Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Took tea at 7 o'clock and at 9 o'clock again got underway with a light breeze <span class=highlight_yellow>from about</span> SSE which carried us down to Southend when  we let go our anchor for the night at &frac12; past 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Discharged</span> a couple of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; turned in at &frac14; to 12 o'clock. Quite ready for a good snooze.</p>\r\n<p>Should the wind continue from the eastward our intention is to go on shore &amp; spend the day at Southend.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":533,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/12/555
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":555,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 7 o'clock. Every appearance of being a very after a thick foggy night. Wind about East. After breakfast Bob Marsh &amp; I went on shore to endeavour to get each a straw hat, but did not succeed. Returned to the ship again before 10 o'clock. During our absence they had got her under way. At low water having had a fresh breeze from the E to ESE. We brought up abreast of Canvey Island, off Leigh at &frac12; past 2 o'clock. Had our dinner which <span class=highlight_yellow>was</span> devoured with a pretty considerable appetite.  Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Took tea at 7 o'clock and at 9 o'clock again got underway with a light breeze <span class=highlight_yellow>from about</span> SSE which carried us down to Southend when  we let go our anchor for the night at &frac12; past 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Discharged</span> a couple of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; turned in at &frac14; to 12 o'clock. Quite ready for a good snooze.</p>\r\n<p>Should the wind continue from the eastward our intention is to go on shore &amp; spend the day at Southend.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":533,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/13/556
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
20:13:19.069 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/13/556
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/14/557
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":557,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>During the night it has blown a steady breeze, fair for us &amp; we have gone along steadily &amp; pleasant at about the rate of 6 knots. After a good snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Not a cloud to be seen, with a find steady &amp; refreshing breeze from SW by W. Steady sails still continued set. After breakfast read the morning service &amp; lessons. Passed through a large fleet of Dutch fishing boats, many of them came near &amp; wished us much to buy some fish. Dined between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock &amp; Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Only one more left, which we have determined, if possible, to keep for next Sunday. We have not averaged this day more than 4 knots an hour.</p>\r\n<p>Started a ENE course. The master &amp; mate at 12 o'clock took their observation on latitude 53.8 North Long 3.30. Distance from the Sunk, run since 6 last evening 111 miles. Changed our course to NE.</p>\r\n<p>At 9 o'clock took our rum &amp; water &amp; afterwards being a most beautiful moonlit night, we walked the deck for &frac12; an hour &amp; at &frac12; past 10 o'clock turned in, at which time we had a better breeze &amp; had increased our rate to about 6 knots.</p>\r\n<p><span class=highlight_yellow>Robecia</span> Filtering Apparatus answered admirably yielding an ample supply of most beautiful clear &amp; pure water, from the filthy Thames mud</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":535,"lock":null}
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/14/557
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":557,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>During the night it has blown a steady breeze, fair for us &amp; we have gone along steadily &amp; pleasant at about the rate of 6 knots. After a good snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Not a cloud to be seen, with a find steady &amp; refreshing breeze from SW by W. Steady sails still continued set. After breakfast read the morning service &amp; lessons. Passed through a large fleet of Dutch fishing boats, many of them came near &amp; wished us much to buy some fish. Dined between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock &amp; Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Only one more left, which we have determined, if possible, to keep for next Sunday. We have not averaged this day more than 4 knots an hour.</p>\r\n<p>Started a ENE course. The master &amp; mate at 12 o'clock took their observation on latitude 53.8 North Long 3.30. Distance from the Sunk, run since 6 last evening 111 miles. Changed our course to NE.</p>\r\n<p>At 9 o'clock took our rum &amp; water &amp; afterwards being a most beautiful moonlit night, we walked the deck for &frac12; an hour &amp; at &frac12; past 10 o'clock turned in, at which time we had a better breeze &amp; had increased our rate to about 6 knots.</p>\r\n<p><span class=highlight_yellow>Robecia</span> Filtering Apparatus answered admirably yielding an ample supply of most beautiful clear &amp; pure water, from the filthy Thames mud</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":535,"lock":null}
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/15/558
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":558,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 6 o'clock. Found that J Galbraith had kept his watch last night for the first time. Have had a fair breeze all night from the SW. Continuing our course NE with steering sail set at the rate of 6 knots. Got fishing line out but could not catch nothing. The ship going too fast through the water.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I employed ourselves in painting the skiff &amp; making her decent to go to shore in at <span class=highlight_yellow>Elsinecra</span>.</p>\r\n<p>At 12 o'clock the Captain took his observation lat 55.1 long: 4.15 having been 112 miles since 12<sup>th</sup>  yesterday &amp; 123 from our departure.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner Bob &amp; I took a single glass of grog &amp; again set to work to finish painting the skiff. In the evening the wind changed to South. Got trysail set &amp; main tack down. At 9 o'clock had a glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; turned in</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":536,"lock":null}
20:13:19.070 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/15/558
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":558,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 6 o'clock. Found that J Galbraith had kept his watch last night for the first time. Have had a fair breeze all night from the SW. Continuing our course NE with steering sail set at the rate of 6 knots. Got fishing line out but could not catch nothing. The ship going too fast through the water.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I employed ourselves in painting the skiff &amp; making her decent to go to shore in at <span class=highlight_yellow>Elsinecra</span>.</p>\r\n<p>At 12 o'clock the Captain took his observation lat 55.1 long: 4.15 having been 112 miles since 12<sup>th</sup>  yesterday &amp; 123 from our departure.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner Bob &amp; I took a single glass of grog &amp; again set to work to finish painting the skiff. In the evening the wind changed to South. Got trysail set &amp; main tack down. At 9 o'clock had a glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; turned in</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":536,"lock":null}
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/559
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":559,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up a little after 7 o'clock. Had a fresh breeze all night from the southward. The ship making 8 knots per hour. A continuance of most beautiful weather. At 12 o'clock took observation Lat:57.4 long: 7.17 having   made 101 miles in the last 24 hours, making 284 since our departure. Employed part of the morning in painting the skiff. At near 2 o'clock saw land on our starboard bow. Off Jutland. The wind, northeast fair, is now against ...</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":537,"lock":null}
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/559
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":559,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up a little after 7 o'clock. Had a fresh breeze all night from the southward. The ship making 8 knots per hour. A continuance of most beautiful weather. At 12 o'clock took observation Lat:57.4 long: 7.17 having   made 101 miles in the last 24 hours, making 284 since our departure. Employed part of the morning in painting the skiff. At near 2 o'clock saw land on our starboard bow. Off Jutland. The wind, northeast fair, is now against ...</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":537,"lock":null}
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/560
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":560,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... SE &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>us</span>. The wind getting to SSE took in studding sails &amp; stood <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of to Norway coast which we saw between 4 &amp; 5 o'clock. Had our fishing lines out all this morning but only caught two gurnets.</p>\r\n<p>A little before 5 o'clock seeing it very black to windward &amp; a squall coming, stowed <span class=highlight_yellow>t</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>galla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span><span class=highlight_yellow>d</span> sails &amp; lowered topsails upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>Cep</span> just as the squall took us, very heavy, but little rain. It lasted about &frac14; of an hour. Got topsails hoisted again single reefed. Wind seemed much inclined to change. At one time we had it to our great delight from the westward where we squared our yards, but to our inexpressible annoyance had scarcely done when it returned to the old quarter.</p>\r\n<p>During this evening a large fleet of loaded vessels upwards of 80 sail from the Baltic passed us as we let out both reefs. Walked the deck until 10 o'clock when we had our glass of comfort &amp; turned in. Little wind from about the south</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":538,"lock":null}
20:13:19.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/16/560
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":560,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... SE &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>us</span>. The wind getting to SSE took in studding sails &amp; stood <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of to Norway coast which we saw between 4 &amp; 5 o'clock. Had our fishing lines out all this morning but only caught two gurnets.</p>\r\n<p>A little before 5 o'clock seeing it very black to windward &amp; a squall coming, stowed <span class=highlight_yellow>t</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>galla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span><span class=highlight_yellow>d</span> sails &amp; lowered topsails upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>Cep</span> just as the squall took us, very heavy, but little rain. It lasted about &frac14; of an hour. Got topsails hoisted again single reefed. Wind seemed much inclined to change. At one time we had it to our great delight from the westward where we squared our yards, but to our inexpressible annoyance had scarcely done when it returned to the old quarter.</p>\r\n<p>During this evening a large fleet of loaded vessels upwards of 80 sail from the Baltic passed us as we let out both reefs. Walked the deck until 10 o'clock when we had our glass of comfort &amp; turned in. Little wind from about the south</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":538,"lock":null}
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/17/561
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":561,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>After a regular good &amp; sound snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>During the night slight shower with very little wind. To our great joy when we looked up through the cabin sky light, we saw the yards square &amp; studding sails set. Wind WNW. After breakfast &amp; commenced a heavy rain, which continued until 12 o'clock. Saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Shaw</span> light at 2 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Employed myself in painting the ship's name upon the skiff &amp; the carpenter finishing painting the underside. Bob Marsh being too lazy to complete his work.</p>\r\n<p>Wind continuing favourable &amp; a fine fresh breeze going 5 or 6 knots. At 9 o'clock saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Trindlew</span> &amp; the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Lcefsoe</span> bearing NW by W, distant 5 or 6 miles.</p>\r\n<p>Took our warm glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; got to bed before 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Looking</span> the sea evidently rising.</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":539,"lock":null}
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/17/561
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":561,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>After a regular good &amp; sound snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>During the night slight shower with very little wind. To our great joy when we looked up through the cabin sky light, we saw the yards square &amp; studding sails set. Wind WNW. After breakfast &amp; commenced a heavy rain, which continued until 12 o'clock. Saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Shaw</span> light at 2 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Employed myself in painting the ship's name upon the skiff &amp; the carpenter finishing painting the underside. Bob Marsh being too lazy to complete his work.</p>\r\n<p>Wind continuing favourable &amp; a fine fresh breeze going 5 or 6 knots. At 9 o'clock saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Trindlew</span> &amp; the island of <span class=highlight_yellow>Lcefsoe</span> bearing NW by W, distant 5 or 6 miles.</p>\r\n<p>Took our warm glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; got to bed before 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Looking</span> the sea evidently rising.</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":539,"lock":null}
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/18/562
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":562,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out about 8 o'clock. This morning after a rather restless night. At 11 o'clock last night it began to blow heavy with sea making the wind suddenly changed to the SE much to our disappointment &amp; increased to a regular squall. Studding sails taken in &amp; top gallent sails stowed. Between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock am Bob &amp; I not being able to sleep, from the motion of the ship &amp; precarious articles of furniture tumbling about the cabin, clothed ourselves &amp; went upon deck. Then about day break found the people employed in double reefing <span class=highlight_yellow>the</span> topsail &amp; taking one reef in the main-top-sail blowing tremendously hard but clear weather. A little after 2 o'clock saw the sun rise which amply <span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ewarded</span> us from our want of rest, a grand &amp; magnificent sight. This morning particularly so. Old sol seemed to have an immediate influence over the wind &amp; waves, for from the time he made his appearance, they gradually lulled. Before 3 o'clock, Bob &amp; I again turned in &amp; had a sound snooze until 8 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>The master informed us that at 6 o'clock it being almost calm. The wind again suddenly changed to the SW but although fair for us for the present, we make but little way, there being a current &amp; hard sea against us. It appeared there are no tides here &amp; in the Baltic &amp; which ever way the wind is, the current sets. Before we went to our roost again at 3 o'clock we saw the <span class=highlight_yellow>Riddigen</span> lighthouse on the Norway coast, beaming <span class=highlight_yellow>mainly</span> SE, distant about 5 miles according to my calculation. Before breakfast set top gallent sail &amp; let out the reefs of topsails. At 10 o'clock the wind got to the westward. In the afternoon set front-top-mast studding sail. It about 5 o'clock the wind getting from the NNW ...</p>","pageId":740,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":540,"lock":null}
20:13:19.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/18/562
