20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":547,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":5,"sequence":4.0000,"text":"<figure>\r\n  <img src=\"images/img2891-image-copenhagen-east.png\" width=\"600px\" />\r\n  <figcaption>Copenhagen east</figcaption>\r\n</figure>","pageId":null,"type":null,"imageId":null,"marqueeId":null,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/5/548
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":548,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":5,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance  of fine weather. Wind NW. Mr Lee drove me into the city. Walked down to the ship upon Dowland's Ways. The painters finished painting the cabin &amp; busy with the sides. Made a port hole into the state room. Moored the ship down in Duke Shore &amp; got ballast. Took boat to the tower &amp; walked from thence to  Mr Lees to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":526,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/5/548
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":548,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":5,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance  of fine weather. Wind NW. Mr Lee drove me into the city. Walked down to the ship upon Dowland's Ways. The painters finished painting the cabin &amp; busy with the sides. Made a port hole into the state room. Moored the ship down in Duke Shore &amp; got ballast. Took boat to the tower &amp; walked from thence to  Mr Lees to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":526,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/6/549
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":549,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning &amp; a fresh breeze from NW. Walked into the city with William. Got a new hat at Olipharts. Called for Bob Marsh &amp; we afterwards went by water to the Hope. Walked home to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":527,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/6/549
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":549,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning &amp; a fresh breeze from NW. Walked into the city with William. Got a new hat at Olipharts. Called for Bob Marsh &amp; we afterwards went by water to the Hope. Walked home to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":527,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/7/550
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":550,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day, but weather cooler. Wind NE. I went to the Roman Catholic Chapel in Warwick Street &amp; much pleased with the ringing. Went to the park, met Bob Marsh &amp; proceeded to Kensington Gardens. A great crowd of fashionable company there &amp; many beautiful women. Dined at Mr Lees.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":528,"lock":null}
20:13:19.091 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/7/550
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":550,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day, but weather cooler. Wind NE. I went to the Roman Catholic Chapel in Warwick Street &amp; much pleased with the ringing. Went to the park, met Bob Marsh &amp; proceeded to Kensington Gardens. A great crowd of fashionable company there &amp; many beautiful women. Dined at Mr Lees.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":528,"lock":null}
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/8/2303
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2303,"version":6,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Petersburg&nbsp;voyage&nbsp;commences</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2243,"lock":null}
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/8/2303
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2303,"version":6,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Petersburg&nbsp;voyage&nbsp;commences</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2243,"lock":null}
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/8/551
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":551,"version":1,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Wind NW. A good deal of rain after a breakfast. Walked into the city. Brought a filtering machine for the ship. Gave Stephenson &pound;25.  Settled the freight account with William &amp; received the balance &pound;30.4/-. Called at the Russian consul for passports.  Dined at Richmonds. Hard J Jobling Chambers &amp; there. When I got home, found J Gilbraith had arrived.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":529,"lock":null}
20:13:19.092 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/8/551
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":551,"version":1,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Wind NW. A good deal of rain after a breakfast. Walked into the city. Brought a filtering machine for the ship. Gave Stephenson &pound;25.  Settled the freight account with William &amp; received the balance &pound;30.4/-. Called at the Russian consul for passports.  Dined at Richmonds. Hard J Jobling Chambers &amp; there. When I got home, found J Gilbraith had arrived.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":529,"lock":null}
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/9/552
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":552,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but rather dull. Wind NNE. <span class=highlight_yellow>Later</span> J Galbraith &amp; I took a coach from Nottingham Place for the city with our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> the ship's boat with four hands at Billingsgate to carry all our things to the ship. <del>Called upon the Russian Consol.</del> The party with one Vinccent carrying &frac12; dozen wine with us took a waterman at the Customs House &amp; went down to the ship to dinner. In the evening attended Greenwich Fair which was not so crowded as I have seen it &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>letto</span><span class=highlight_yellow> run</span>. I called upon G Watts. William, Lee &amp; Vincent left us &amp; Bob Marsh J.G. &amp; I returned to the ship a little after 10 o'clock to sleep. Bob &amp; I smoked a cloud, wet both eyes &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":530,"lock":null}
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/6/9/552
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":552,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but rather dull. Wind NNE. <span class=highlight_yellow>Later</span> J Galbraith &amp; I took a coach from Nottingham Place for the city with our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> the ship's boat with four hands at Billingsgate to carry all our things to the ship. <del>Called upon the Russian Consol.</del> The party with one Vinccent carrying &frac12; dozen wine with us took a waterman at the Customs House &amp; went down to the ship to dinner. In the evening attended Greenwich Fair which was not so crowded as I have seen it &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>letto</span><span class=highlight_yellow> run</span>. I called upon G Watts. William, Lee &amp; Vincent left us &amp; Bob Marsh J.G. &amp; I returned to the ship a little after 10 o'clock to sleep. Bob &amp; I smoked a cloud, wet both eyes &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":530,"lock":null}
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/1/584
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":584,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another fine morning. The heat not quite so oppressive, but still warm enough.</p>\r\n<p>After wasting some time, the officers came on board from what they call the Potash &amp; taking a list of our luggage, describing each article &amp; accompanied by us carried everything on shore to the Custom House. Here after wasting a considerable time, until their <i>excellences</i> the collector &amp; clerk thought proper to move their stumps, the whole of our luggage was examined &amp; every article in our boxes turned topsy-turvy. We mentioned our intention of going to Petersburg, and that we should want our luggage. They wished us mesely to take out a shirt or two &amp; a few things, but we told them there was nothing there but what we absolutely wanted &amp; could not do without &amp; at last after some hesitation, they gave us our orders for the whole to be delivered to us the next morning. Having me sea sheets &amp; on board a white shirt with a few other things stowed away, I was not so badly off by our boxes being so many days under seal, but poor Bob Marsh had not been quite so provident &amp; was obliged to continue wearing a shirt &amp; which he had had on ...</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":559,"lock":null}
20:13:19.093 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/1/584
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":584,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":1,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another fine morning. The heat not quite so oppressive, but still warm enough.</p>\r\n<p>After wasting some time, the officers came on board from what they call the Potash &amp; taking a list of our luggage, describing each article &amp; accompanied by us carried everything on shore to the Custom House. Here after wasting a considerable time, until their <i>excellences</i> the collector &amp; clerk thought proper to move their stumps, the whole of our luggage was examined &amp; every article in our boxes turned topsy-turvy. We mentioned our intention of going to Petersburg, and that we should want our luggage. They wished us mesely to take out a shirt or two &amp; a few things, but we told them there was nothing there but what we absolutely wanted &amp; could not do without &amp; at last after some hesitation, they gave us our orders for the whole to be delivered to us the next morning. Having me sea sheets &amp; on board a white shirt with a few other things stowed away, I was not so badly off by our boxes being so many days under seal, but poor Bob Marsh had not been quite so provident &amp; was obliged to continue wearing a shirt &amp; which he had had on ...</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":559,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/1/585
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":585,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":1,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... several days before. From the Customs House we went to Mr Cramps, an Englishman, interpreter to the Russian Admiral from whom we got fresh papers, paying him 5 Rubles.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob John G<font color=#BFBFBF>albraith</font> &amp; I landed upon the mole head which we thought would be the coolest place &amp; here lounged the whole of the afternoon amusing ourselves by watching the departure of ships &amp; the passage to &amp; fro for the various boats &amp; craft of the <span class=highlight_yellow>haleires</span>. During this time Stephenson was on shore buying Calkwood for broken stowage, which I have determined to load upon my own account, the freights here for it being low. After tea, wrote a letter to William to go buy some vessel bound to London &amp; then we took our usual evening's lounger about the town, had our grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":560,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/1/585
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":585,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":1,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... several days before. From the Customs House we went to Mr Cramps, an Englishman, interpreter to the Russian Admiral from whom we got fresh papers, paying him 5 Rubles.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob John G<font color=#BFBFBF>albraith</font> &amp; I landed upon the mole head which we thought would be the coolest place &amp; here lounged the whole of the afternoon amusing ourselves by watching the departure of ships &amp; the passage to &amp; fro for the various boats &amp; craft of the <span class=highlight_yellow>haleires</span>. During this time Stephenson was on shore buying Calkwood for broken stowage, which I have determined to load upon my own account, the freights here for it being low. After tea, wrote a letter to William to go buy some vessel bound to London &amp; then we took our usual evening's lounger about the town, had our grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":560,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/10/605
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":605,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>The weather still continues particularly fine. Did not go out this morning till after 11 o'clock when Bob &amp; I went to the <span class=highlight_yellow>Aliex</span> officer for our permit of residence for which we each had to pay 10 rubbles. We then went to the museum where we saw a great variety of minerals <span class=highlight_yellow>surgeal</span> preparations, stuffed animals of all sorts, a number of things in ivory beautifully turned, with the lathes all the workmanship of Peter the Great.</p>\r\n<p>Dined with Mr Dye where we met a Mr Atkinson, Mr Bush &amp; Mr Linsey. Mr Dye kindly asked us to dine with him again at his country house that we might see some fete that is to take place in the neighbourhood. Left Mr Dye's early being foreign port night. Took a stroll with Mr Lindsey &amp; called upon a friend of his. Got home a little after 10 o'clock, finished a glass of grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":573,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/10/605
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":605,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>The weather still continues particularly fine. Did not go out this morning till after 11 o'clock when Bob &amp; I went to the <span class=highlight_yellow>Aliex</span> officer for our permit of residence for which we each had to pay 10 rubbles. We then went to the museum where we saw a great variety of minerals <span class=highlight_yellow>surgeal</span> preparations, stuffed animals of all sorts, a number of things in ivory beautifully turned, with the lathes all the workmanship of Peter the Great.</p>\r\n<p>Dined with Mr Dye where we met a Mr Atkinson, Mr Bush &amp; Mr Linsey. Mr Dye kindly asked us to dine with him again at his country house that we might see some fete that is to take place in the neighbourhood. Left Mr Dye's early being foreign port night. Took a stroll with Mr Lindsey &amp; called upon a friend of his. Got home a little after 10 o'clock, finished a glass of grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":573,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/11/2315
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
20:13:19.094 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.095 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/11/2315
20:13:19.095 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
