20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2310,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":4.0000,"text":"<p>... took a long stroll down the New <span class=highlight_yellow>Ky</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>Prosped</span>, the fashionable &amp; finest street in Pitusby. It is about 3 <span class=highlight_yellow>wests</span> in length &amp; having <span class=highlight_yellow>trus</span> on each side between the froth pavement &amp; road which adds much to its beauty. Returned to our lodgings about 11 o'clock completely knocked up &amp; foot sore.</p>","pageId":749,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2250,"lock":null}
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/2/586
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":586,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out very early. The morning having to be on board the steam boat at 9 o'clock. After breakfast went on shore to the Custom House, got all our luggage &amp; an officer to accompany us to the steamer or otherwise it would have had to be searched again before we put it on board. The <span class=highlight_yellow>gunr</span> desk &amp; we left at Mr Williamsons a very respectable shopkeeper's until our return. At 9 o'clock we <span class=highlight_yellow>sent</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>asked</span> for Petersburg having a beautiful morning for our ... </p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":561,"lock":null}
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/2/586
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":586,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out very early. The morning having to be on board the steam boat at 9 o'clock. After breakfast went on shore to the Custom House, got all our luggage &amp; an officer to accompany us to the steamer or otherwise it would have had to be searched again before we put it on board. The <span class=highlight_yellow>gunr</span> desk &amp; we left at Mr Williamsons a very respectable shopkeeper's until our return. At 9 o'clock we <span class=highlight_yellow>sent</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>asked</span> for Petersburg having a beautiful morning for our ... </p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":561,"lock":null}
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/2/587
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":587,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... you behold some Palace or public building which for grandeur &amp; beauty &amp; magnificence are far exceed my powers of description. The Winter Palace is by far the finest building of the sort I ever saw. Its size is immense, as for St James compound to it, is like a peg size compared with <span class=highlight_yellow>Wanstrand</span>. In fact London is not to be mentioned with Petersburg &amp; if I mistake not Regent Street &amp; the other new streets now forming are bad copies of the streets &amp; buildings in Petersburg.</p>\r\n<p>The admiralty is a noble building of great extent having three sides is built that next to the river being open &amp; the square forming a fine yard there they build their men-of-war.</p>\r\n<p>Next to &amp; above the Admiralty stands the Emperors Winter Palace. A very splendid building, having four sides &amp; forming a hollow square exceedingly large &amp; commanding though it is, the Hermitage.</p>\r\n<p>Here I must stop for I find it impossible to enter into a complete description of every place &amp; building one sees, they are so numerous.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I attended Change about 4 o'clock &amp; a splendid building it is &amp; I should imagine one of the finest rooms in Europe. Here I was introduced by Mr Dye to our merchants Mr Hubband &amp; Gillibrand. Offered 30 tons of copper for ballast at 10/-. Wrote to Stephenson about it. Dined at &frac12; past 5 o'clock at Mr Wilson where we met about 121 others specifically I should thank American Captain Did mot much like rather one company or over dinner. In the cool of the evening we ...</p>","pageId":749,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":562,"lock":null}
20:13:19.105 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
