20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":601,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":4.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance of hot weather. The first thing after breakfast was to go with Mr Lindsay to the Hermitage, the interior of which is most splendid. A suit of apartments throughout communication with each other &amp; every room filled with the paintings of all the old &amp; first masters &amp; in the centre of almost every room was a vase of <span class=highlight_yellow>imimeange</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>sege</span> &amp; most beautifully executed. This backing forms a square, as indeed do all the Palaces &amp; in the centre  upon the roof of the first floor they have made a very pretty garden, a <span class=highlight_yellow>retired</span> spot for the <span class=highlight_yellow>perpermeal</span> family &amp; the trees in it so large in each a place. Attached to the Hermitage, the Emperor has a very <span class=highlight_yellow>neat</span> private theatre which we ...</p>","pageId":752,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":569,"lock":null}
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/8/602
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":602,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<p>... did not see to advantage being under repairs. It would require two or three days to examine manually all that this Plaice contains. The admirer of the fine arts, the antiquarian &amp; mineralogist may find many days employed in this building. From this after having had a lunch, we crossed the water to the &quot;Hotel de Miner&quot; where we spent two hours very agreeably in examining the models of all sorts of machinery. The Siberian mines of gold &amp; silver, the <span class=highlight_yellow>glenichs</span> specimens, mineral produced by them. One specimen of malachite the largest &amp; finest in the world, weighing a ton &amp; a half. It is said the Emperor has offered its weight in silver some say in gold &amp; when the celebrated professor Homburg first entered the room, he was in such <span class=highlight_yellow>ecstasy</span> at the sight of it, that he actually dropped to his knees and was <span class=highlight_yellow>helped at</span>. Under the gardens of the institution they have made a mine, a representation of the Serbian gold &amp; silver mines. It is of considerable extent the same size &amp; the different stratas <span class=highlight_yellow>sh</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>w</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span>. Each having a candle, we were led through the various windings of this interesting place &amp; much satisfied. We also had a fine view of the city at the top of the observatory.  At this institution there are a great number of cadets educated for the Corp of Sappers &amp; mines.</p>\r\n<p>Mr Thilkey who was to drive us to his country house about 9 miles down ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":570,"lock":null}
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/8/602
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":602,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<p>... did not see to advantage being under repairs. It would require two or three days to examine manually all that this Plaice contains. The admirer of the fine arts, the antiquarian &amp; mineralogist may find many days employed in this building. From this after having had a lunch, we crossed the water to the &quot;Hotel de Miner&quot; where we spent two hours very agreeably in examining the models of all sorts of machinery. The Siberian mines of gold &amp; silver, the <span class=highlight_yellow>glenichs</span> specimens, mineral produced by them. One specimen of malachite the largest &amp; finest in the world, weighing a ton &amp; a half. It is said the Emperor has offered its weight in silver some say in gold &amp; when the celebrated professor Homburg first entered the room, he was in such <span class=highlight_yellow>ecstasy</span> at the sight of it, that he actually dropped to his knees and was <span class=highlight_yellow>helped at</span>. Under the gardens of the institution they have made a mine, a representation of the Serbian gold &amp; silver mines. It is of considerable extent the same size &amp; the different stratas <span class=highlight_yellow>sh</span><span class=highlight_yellow>o</span><span class=highlight_yellow>w</span><span class=highlight_yellow>n</span>. Each having a candle, we were led through the various windings of this interesting place &amp; much satisfied. We also had a fine view of the city at the top of the observatory.  At this institution there are a great number of cadets educated for the Corp of Sappers &amp; mines.</p>\r\n<p>Mr Thilkey who was to drive us to his country house about 9 miles down ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":570,"lock":null}
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/2314
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
20:13:19.124 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/2314
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/603
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":603,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fair morning. Mr Linsey, his friend, Bob &amp; I started at 8 o'clock in a <span class=highlight_yellow>barrack</span> for the Imperial Palace of <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Pavhousky</span>, about 22 <span class=highlight_yellow>versts</span> off, where we arrived in about 2 &frac12; hours. <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> is a summer palace &amp; surpasses everything we have yet seen for brilliance &amp; splendour. It is grand &amp; magnificent beyond the possibility of just description. Every room is most richly guild with the finest gold and I showed surprise at an immense expenditure at one time. The roof &amp; all the exterior ornaments were done in the same way, but &lsquo;tis said that Catherine when much in want of money, made an agreement with a rich Jew, who advanced her a certain sum, for the purpose of stripping the gold off the exterior. Nothing can exceed the attention of the Chapple. The <span class=highlight_yellow>furniture</span> is exceedingly rich &amp; handsome &amp; great taste is displayed ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":571,"lock":null}
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/603
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":603,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fair morning. Mr Linsey, his friend, Bob &amp; I started at 8 o'clock in a <span class=highlight_yellow>barrack</span> for the Imperial Palace of <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>Pavhousky</span>, about 22 <span class=highlight_yellow>versts</span> off, where we arrived in about 2 &frac12; hours. <span class=highlight_yellow>Sarskoi</span> is a summer palace &amp; surpasses everything we have yet seen for brilliance &amp; splendour. It is grand &amp; magnificent beyond the possibility of just description. Every room is most richly guild with the finest gold and I showed surprise at an immense expenditure at one time. The roof &amp; all the exterior ornaments were done in the same way, but &lsquo;tis said that Catherine when much in want of money, made an agreement with a rich Jew, who advanced her a certain sum, for the purpose of stripping the gold off the exterior. Nothing can exceed the attention of the Chapple. The <span class=highlight_yellow>furniture</span> is exceedingly rich &amp; handsome &amp; great taste is displayed ...</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":571,"lock":null}
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/7/9/604
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":604,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... throughout. The grand dining room is a noble apartments &amp; of great size and there is great comfort neatness &amp; cleanliness in the Emperor &amp; Empress's private apartments. The grounds which are very extensive being about the diameter of five miles, are laid out with great taste &amp; judgement &amp; kept in high order &amp; beautifully interspaces with wood &amp; water. In the plantations is a summer or private dining room built by Catherine &amp; elaborated by her making it the scene of her private armoires. In the centre of the building, upon the finest floor is the dining room &amp; that <span class=highlight_yellow>night</span> not having the attendance of servants, the table was worked by machinery &amp; upon a bell being rung, the whole descended to a room below &amp; there replenished, or if merely a clean plate was wanted, the plate stand, alone went down. But of each corner of the dining room was a cabinet intended no doubt for beds or couches. But when we were there, the whole was under repair &amp; consequently not furnished.</p>\r\n<p>The Palace of Jarskoi not being large enough for the whole of the family, another upon a smaller &amp; less magnificent scale was built in the grounds. A plain but handsome mansion. There are also a number of houses at a short distance from the Palace, built after the Chinese fashion forming a small village ...</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":572,"lock":null}
20:13:19.125 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
