20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":652,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. In the middle of the night it became quite calm &amp; the wind changing to the eastward enabled us to get underway. Between 8 &amp; 9 o'clock &amp; to leave a place that I never again wish to see under similar circumstances. As the wind changed with the moon, it will carry us safely to England ...</p>","pageId":765,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":614,"lock":null}
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/14/653
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":653,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":14,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... should it continue. At 6 o'clock passed the Scaw Light with a fresh breeze &amp; every appearance of it increasing, the sea making very much. Turned <font color=#BFBFBF>in </font>at about our usual hour</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":615,"lock":null}
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/14/653
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":653,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":14,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... should it continue. At 6 o'clock passed the Scaw Light with a fresh breeze &amp; every appearance of it increasing, the sea making very much. Turned <font color=#BFBFBF>in </font>at about our usual hour</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":615,"lock":null}
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/15/2321
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2321,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... the stateroom scuttle light, wet my bed &amp; I did not expect to have had one to sleep in tonight, indeed had the gale continued I should not have cared about it, but I contrived by placing canvas between the bed and sides of the vessel to fortify it pretty well from the wet &amp; thus gained a better nights rest than I anticipated.</p>\r\n<p>My face beginning to swell again, but no pain.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2261,"lock":null}
20:13:19.129 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/15/2321
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2321,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... the stateroom scuttle light, wet my bed &amp; I did not expect to have had one to sleep in tonight, indeed had the gale continued I should not have cared about it, but I contrived by placing canvas between the bed and sides of the vessel to fortify it pretty well from the wet &amp; thus gained a better nights rest than I anticipated.</p>\r\n<p>My face beginning to swell again, but no pain.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2261,"lock":null}
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/15/654
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":654,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning at a little before 8 o'clock after a very boisterous night, the wind having increased to more than half a gale with a tremendous sea, right aft so that we made good way, though at the sacrifice of our comfort. Everything above &amp; below being wet &amp; in confusion. At 5 o'clock this morning a tremendous heavy sea pooped us, stove &amp; carried away our jolly boat &amp; alarmed us so much fearing it had stove in the dark lights &amp; if it had so happened we of course should have been waterlogged. Knowing the delicate state of the <span class=highlight_yellow>archboard</span>, where she was making a great deal of water, the fear of another heavy sea striking us kept me in a very uncomfortable state the whole of the day. Towards evening however both wind &amp; sea fell considerably. We have been going today from 6&frac12;  to 7 knots. Ship laboured very much &amp; made a deal of water. We consider we have got this evening considerably to the westward of the Naze of Norway, say 50 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great deal of water having come into ...</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":616,"lock":null}
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/15/654
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":654,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning at a little before 8 o'clock after a very boisterous night, the wind having increased to more than half a gale with a tremendous sea, right aft so that we made good way, though at the sacrifice of our comfort. Everything above &amp; below being wet &amp; in confusion. At 5 o'clock this morning a tremendous heavy sea pooped us, stove &amp; carried away our jolly boat &amp; alarmed us so much fearing it had stove in the dark lights &amp; if it had so happened we of course should have been waterlogged. Knowing the delicate state of the <span class=highlight_yellow>archboard</span>, where she was making a great deal of water, the fear of another heavy sea striking us kept me in a very uncomfortable state the whole of the day. Towards evening however both wind &amp; sea fell considerably. We have been going today from 6&frac12;  to 7 knots. Ship laboured very much &amp; made a deal of water. We consider we have got this evening considerably to the westward of the Naze of Norway, say 50 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great deal of water having come into ...</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":616,"lock":null}
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/16/655
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":655,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>More moderate weather. Wind NE. A fine breeze. Ship going about 4 knots. Let reefs out of the top sails, set top gallants sails, <span class=highlight_yellow>square mainsail free</span>, <span class=highlight_yellow>topmasts </span><span class=highlight_yellow>liering</span><span class=highlight_yellow> sail</span> &amp; gib.</p>\r\n<p>Read the duty &amp; Lessons of the day.</p>\r\n<p>The mainsail being split, unbent it &amp; got up another. The breeze rather freshened. This afternoon at NNE &amp; B by E. In the evening took a reef in each topsail &amp; stowed topgallant sails.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 turned in, being by my calculation about 100 miles from the Land</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":617,"lock":null}
20:13:19.130 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/16/655
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":655,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>More moderate weather. Wind NE. A fine breeze. Ship going about 4 knots. Let reefs out of the top sails, set top gallants sails, <span class=highlight_yellow>square mainsail free</span>, <span class=highlight_yellow>topmasts </span><span class=highlight_yellow>liering</span><span class=highlight_yellow> sail</span> &amp; gib.</p>\r\n<p>Read the duty &amp; Lessons of the day.</p>\r\n<p>The mainsail being split, unbent it &amp; got up another. The breeze rather freshened. This afternoon at NNE &amp; B by E. In the evening took a reef in each topsail &amp; stowed topgallant sails.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 turned in, being by my calculation about 100 miles from the Land</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":617,"lock":null}
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/17/656
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":656,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning much disappointed at not finding ourselves within 20 miles of land, which we should have been had the wind continued as it was last night. But unfortunately it has changed in the night to NW &amp; NW by W &amp; we are now only going 3 knots. The weather fine, but cloudy. Hope we shall have a change before night for I'm anxious to land at Shields.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":618,"lock":null}
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/17/656
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":656,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning much disappointed at not finding ourselves within 20 miles of land, which we should have been had the wind continued as it was last night. But unfortunately it has changed in the night to NW &amp; NW by W &amp; we are now only going 3 knots. The weather fine, but cloudy. Hope we shall have a change before night for I'm anxious to land at Shields.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":618,"lock":null}
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/17/657
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":657,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>We are now about 80 miles from the land, returning about SW. Continued this course. Ship making a deal of water.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":619,"lock":null}
20:13:19.131 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/17/657
20:13:19.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":657,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>We are now about 80 miles from the land, returning about SW. Continued this course. Ship making a deal of water.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":619,"lock":null}
20:13:19.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/18/658
20:13:19.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":658,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull couldy morning with rain fresh breeze etc W by N going only 3 &frac12;  knots. At &frac12; past 6 o'clock being thick hazy weather we made Huntcliff foot at least we concluded it to be so. This at first we thought it Tynemouth. We lay the ship to with her head off.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":620,"lock":null}
20:13:19.132 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/18/658
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":658,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull couldy morning with rain fresh breeze etc W by N going only 3 &frac12;  knots. At &frac12; past 6 o'clock being thick hazy weather we made Huntcliff foot at least we concluded it to be so. This at first we thought it Tynemouth. We lay the ship to with her head off.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":620,"lock":null}
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/19/659
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":659,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>This morning thick weather the land in sight like a fog bank, but clearing away we found it for certain to be Huntcliff foot, bearing west about five miles. During the morning light <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> variable &amp; in the afternoon calm with tremendous heavy rain, thunder &amp; lightning. At night the wind being from the NE light breeze &amp; I very unwell with <span class=highlight_yellow>scumalign</span>. The ship short of Stocks as water coals &amp; pump gear out of order &amp; considering she might reach shields, by daylight, we determined to work to windward &amp; turned in for the night.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":621,"lock":null}
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/19/659
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":659,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>This morning thick weather the land in sight like a fog bank, but clearing away we found it for certain to be Huntcliff foot, bearing west about five miles. During the morning light <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> variable &amp; in the afternoon calm with tremendous heavy rain, thunder &amp; lightning. At night the wind being from the NE light breeze &amp; I very unwell with <span class=highlight_yellow>scumalign</span>. The ship short of Stocks as water coals &amp; pump gear out of order &amp; considering she might reach shields, by daylight, we determined to work to windward &amp; turned in for the night.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":621,"lock":null}
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/2/639
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":639,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at our usual hour. Finer weather, wind NE &amp; strong sea, but still cool. At noon by observation lat:57.8N  Wind WNW. The strong sea preventing us making more than 2 or 3 knots. Read service &amp; lessons for the day. In the afternoon wind NW &amp; blowing fresh, only going from 3 to 4 knots. A few ships in company. Amongst them Bowies Malvina. At 4 o'clock took in top gallants sails. Bob &amp; I very tired of this slow work.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock finished a couple of glasses of warm grog &amp; turned in hoping to sleep better than last night. The tallow casks making a most infernal creaking. Obliged to get the state room port light caulked, a pretty considerable quantity of water having come in &amp; wet my bed</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":601,"lock":null}
20:13:19.133 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/2/639
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":639,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":2,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at our usual hour. Finer weather, wind NE &amp; strong sea, but still cool. At noon by observation lat:57.8N  Wind WNW. The strong sea preventing us making more than 2 or 3 knots. Read service &amp; lessons for the day. In the afternoon wind NW &amp; blowing fresh, only going from 3 to 4 knots. A few ships in company. Amongst them Bowies Malvina. At 4 o'clock took in top gallants sails. Bob &amp; I very tired of this slow work.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock finished a couple of glasses of warm grog &amp; turned in hoping to sleep better than last night. The tallow casks making a most infernal creaking. Obliged to get the state room port light caulked, a pretty considerable quantity of water having come in &amp; wet my bed</p>","pageId":762,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":601,"lock":null}
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/2322
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2322,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... get at them. Bob &amp; I much annoyed having made certain of having a comfortable dinner &amp; bed on show &amp; talked over our plans of future proceedings. He having determined to stay a few days at Shields &amp; take his passage in some other vessel.</p>\r\n<p>The wind &amp; sea being right aft, we soon passed Huntcliff foot again &amp; at 8 o'clock left Flamborough Head bearing west distant 2 or 3 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great many light ships in Burlington Bat as we passed.</p>\r\n<p>Ship made a great deal of water during the day</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2262,"lock":null}
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/2322
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2322,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... get at them. Bob &amp; I much annoyed having made certain of having a comfortable dinner &amp; bed on show &amp; talked over our plans of future proceedings. He having determined to stay a few days at Shields &amp; take his passage in some other vessel.</p>\r\n<p>The wind &amp; sea being right aft, we soon passed Huntcliff foot again &amp; at 8 o'clock left Flamborough Head bearing west distant 2 or 3 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great many light ships in Burlington Bat as we passed.</p>\r\n<p>Ship made a great deal of water during the day</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2262,"lock":null}
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/660
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":660,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock found the ship turning to windward off Marsden Rock blowing hard from N by E. The wind having increased during the night with a high sea. At about 9 o'clock, the breeze increasing &amp; no chance of us reaching Tynemouth of getting on shore. Even if we had, we bore away for London much to our chagrin &amp; disappointment having had Shields Dockwray Square <span class=highlight_yellow>etc</span> in view &amp; not able to ...</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":622,"lock":null}
20:13:19.134 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/20/660
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":660,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out this morning between 5 &amp; 6 o'clock found the ship turning to windward off Marsden Rock blowing hard from N by E. The wind having increased during the night with a high sea. At about 9 o'clock, the breeze increasing &amp; no chance of us reaching Tynemouth of getting on shore. Even if we had, we bore away for London much to our chagrin &amp; disappointment having had Shields Dockwray Square <span class=highlight_yellow>etc</span> in view &amp; not able to ...</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":622,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/21/661
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":661,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. Most moderate weather. Wind N by W night after Studdington set Just passed the Floating light, having made good way during the night. Had a good night's rest, but much plagued with the rheumatism</p>\r\n<p>Passed Cromer at 1 o'clock with less wind Between 8 &amp; 9 o'clock in the evening abreast of Harborough endeavouring to get through the gateway, but falling calm were driven back by the tide. With much trouble got the ship head round to the westward &amp; a light breeze springing up, stood a crop to the land. At 10 o'clock went to bed. Tide turned &amp; the captain intending to work up to Winterton &amp; anchor, the wind being from the SW.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":623,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/21/661
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":661,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":21,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A fine morning. Most moderate weather. Wind N by W night after Studdington set Just passed the Floating light, having made good way during the night. Had a good night's rest, but much plagued with the rheumatism</p>\r\n<p>Passed Cromer at 1 o'clock with less wind Between 8 &amp; 9 o'clock in the evening abreast of Harborough endeavouring to get through the gateway, but falling calm were driven back by the tide. With much trouble got the ship head round to the westward &amp; a light breeze springing up, stood a crop to the land. At 10 o'clock went to bed. Tide turned &amp; the captain intending to work up to Winterton &amp; anchor, the wind being from the SW.</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":623,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/2323
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/2323
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/662
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":662,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Went upon deck at 8 o'clock. Found the ship outside the sands nearly as far as Lowestoft turning to windward with the wind at WSW. It seems that last night soon after we turned in, the wind suddenly got to the W &amp; WNW where they squared the yards, set studdingsails &amp; ran out of harbour gateway.</p>\r\n<p>The wind increasing from the WNW stowed topgallant sails, reefed topsails, afterwards stowed square mainmast &amp; bore away &amp; at 12 o'clock when off Southwold, bore away for Yarmouth, passing through St Nicholas Gateway, where to my inexpressible horror and alarm we nearly struck upon the north end of the Croton Sand, the captain, me, taking the last buoy for the buoy of a wreck in the rocks. The lead was thrown. The mate called out &frac12; 3 then &frac14; 3 &ndash; 3 but when it came to &frac14; less 3 my state fear &amp; agitation, for the safety of the ship, was not to be envied, expecting every moment she would strike &amp; had she, the least that could have happened would most likely have been to deliver the cargo &amp; repair, being already very leaky, but she probably might have gone down in deep water or have drifted on the sand to leeward where the mast probably have been lost. However the next throw of the lead the mate called &frac14; 3 &amp; then &frac12; &amp; 4 fathoms was a wonderful relief to me. Such was agitation that I could almost have cried indeed I wouldn't say that the big tear did not stand in my eye but I ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":624,"lock":null}
20:13:19.135 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/22/662
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":662,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Went upon deck at 8 o'clock. Found the ship outside the sands nearly as far as Lowestoft turning to windward with the wind at WSW. It seems that last night soon after we turned in, the wind suddenly got to the W &amp; WNW where they squared the yards, set studdingsails &amp; ran out of harbour gateway.</p>\r\n<p>The wind increasing from the WNW stowed topgallant sails, reefed topsails, afterwards stowed square mainmast &amp; bore away &amp; at 12 o'clock when off Southwold, bore away for Yarmouth, passing through St Nicholas Gateway, where to my inexpressible horror and alarm we nearly struck upon the north end of the Croton Sand, the captain, me, taking the last buoy for the buoy of a wreck in the rocks. The lead was thrown. The mate called out &frac12; 3 then &frac14; 3 &ndash; 3 but when it came to &frac14; less 3 my state fear &amp; agitation, for the safety of the ship, was not to be envied, expecting every moment she would strike &amp; had she, the least that could have happened would most likely have been to deliver the cargo &amp; repair, being already very leaky, but she probably might have gone down in deep water or have drifted on the sand to leeward where the mast probably have been lost. However the next throw of the lead the mate called &frac14; 3 &amp; then &frac12; &amp; 4 fathoms was a wonderful relief to me. Such was agitation that I could almost have cried indeed I wouldn't say that the big tear did not stand in my eye but I ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":624,"lock":null}
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/663
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":663,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out after a food nights rest at 8 o'clock. The gale as heavy as ever with rain. (Sent top gallant yards driven &amp; struck the mast)</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock finer weather &amp; some appearance of clearing up having had a glimpse of the sun.</p>\r\n<p>Wrote a letter to William &amp; Mr Clark of the Duke of Clarence coming on board. We all went on shore at 12 o'clock to put the letter into the post. Walked about &amp; saw a little of the town of Yarmouth with which I was much pleased. After having some bread &amp; cheese, <span class=highlight_yellow>portion</span> &amp; a glass of grog, we left the jetty about 4 o'clock ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":625,"lock":null}
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/663
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":663,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out after a food nights rest at 8 o'clock. The gale as heavy as ever with rain. (Sent top gallant yards driven &amp; struck the mast)</p>\r\n<p>At 11 o'clock finer weather &amp; some appearance of clearing up having had a glimpse of the sun.</p>\r\n<p>Wrote a letter to William &amp; Mr Clark of the Duke of Clarence coming on board. We all went on shore at 12 o'clock to put the letter into the post. Walked about &amp; saw a little of the town of Yarmouth with which I was much pleased. After having some bread &amp; cheese, <span class=highlight_yellow>portion</span> &amp; a glass of grog, we left the jetty about 4 o'clock ...</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":625,"lock":null}
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/664
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":664,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... reached the ship in about &frac12; a hour after a heavy pull. Still blowing very hard. Mr Clark took tea with us in the evening.  The weather much improved both sea &amp; wind having fallen.</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":626,"lock":null}
20:13:19.136 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/23/664
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":664,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... reached the ship in about &frac12; a hour after a heavy pull. Still blowing very hard. Mr Clark took tea with us in the evening.  The weather much improved both sea &amp; wind having fallen.</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":626,"lock":null}
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/24/665
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":665,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Still blowing very hard from I believe the WSW. About 9 o'clock a most tremendous squall came off the land, a perfect hurricane, enough almost to throw the masts out of the ship. But fortunately it did not continue over &frac12; an hour. It changed the wind however to WNW when a number of the ships immediately began to get under way. Although continuing to blow hard &amp; having a very unsettled appearance.</p>\r\n<p>It was the contention of the master to have gone on shore &amp; brought what previsions &amp; water he wanted but the change of wind &amp; other ships getting under way, colliers like, he must follow the example &amp; at 10 o'clock having first been on board the Duke of <span class=highlight_yellow>Cla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ian</span> &amp; borrowed a bag of bread, we also up anchor &amp; ran out of the gateway. But we had not been long outside before we repented, for it came on a heavy squall, blowing &amp; raining like fury &amp; very thick. We however kept all our canvas standing excepting the mainsail &amp; continued our course to the SW. Towards evening the weather clear &amp; more moderate &amp; at 8 o'clock, having reached Orford <span class=highlight_yellow>refs</span> wind about W by N we bore up &amp; ran into Aldeburgh roads where we brought up for the night in a snug roadstead.</p>\r\n<p>Many other ships following our example. Blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discasted</span> a glass of grog &amp; turned in before 10 o'clock</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":627,"lock":null}
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/24/665
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":665,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Still blowing very hard from I believe the WSW. About 9 o'clock a most tremendous squall came off the land, a perfect hurricane, enough almost to throw the masts out of the ship. But fortunately it did not continue over &frac12; an hour. It changed the wind however to WNW when a number of the ships immediately began to get under way. Although continuing to blow hard &amp; having a very unsettled appearance.</p>\r\n<p>It was the contention of the master to have gone on shore &amp; brought what previsions &amp; water he wanted but the change of wind &amp; other ships getting under way, colliers like, he must follow the example &amp; at 10 o'clock having first been on board the Duke of <span class=highlight_yellow>Cla</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ian</span> &amp; borrowed a bag of bread, we also up anchor &amp; ran out of the gateway. But we had not been long outside before we repented, for it came on a heavy squall, blowing &amp; raining like fury &amp; very thick. We however kept all our canvas standing excepting the mainsail &amp; continued our course to the SW. Towards evening the weather clear &amp; more moderate &amp; at 8 o'clock, having reached Orford <span class=highlight_yellow>refs</span> wind about W by N we bore up &amp; ran into Aldeburgh roads where we brought up for the night in a snug roadstead.</p>\r\n<p>Many other ships following our example. Blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discasted</span> a glass of grog &amp; turned in before 10 o'clock</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":627,"lock":null}
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/25/666
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":666,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine clear morning, but wind not at all abated, about WNW &amp; the night has been so boisterous with rain, that the master &amp; all the vessels in company have not thought it prudent to move. The people employed in sending down the Royals &amp; setting up the short topgallant masts. Seeing about board a ship near us, we were in no little alarm, lest it should be one of the preventative service &amp; were in a devil of a brush to get our goods and challis stowed away. It however proved a false alarm, being only a pilot boat. Got under way at 12 o'clock &amp; turned to windward with a fresh breeze. At 9 o'clock pm brought up abreast of the hook of the Gunfleet for the night</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":628,"lock":null}
20:13:19.137 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/25/666
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":666,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine clear morning, but wind not at all abated, about WNW &amp; the night has been so boisterous with rain, that the master &amp; all the vessels in company have not thought it prudent to move. The people employed in sending down the Royals &amp; setting up the short topgallant masts. Seeing about board a ship near us, we were in no little alarm, lest it should be one of the preventative service &amp; were in a devil of a brush to get our goods and challis stowed away. It however proved a false alarm, being only a pilot boat. Got under way at 12 o'clock &amp; turned to windward with a fresh breeze. At 9 o'clock pm brought up abreast of the hook of the Gunfleet for the night</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":628,"lock":null}
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/26/667
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":667,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>By daylight this morning. They got the ship underway at low water. When we got up at 8 o'clock the weather much pleasanter &amp; continued fine all day. At noon brought up at high water abreast of Whitaker Beason. Wind SW. At 5 pm,  low water, we again got under way, but were much delayed by a ship giving us a foul birth, the consequence was it became dark before we could work through Shoe hole  &amp; see the mouse buoy, where unfortunately it also turned very squally &amp; blowing a gale of wind. We were working towards the nose light &amp; should have got well up but the overhaul of our gib giving way the ship would not steer &amp; we were obliged, though not unwilling by ...</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":629,"lock":null}
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/26/667
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":667,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>By daylight this morning. They got the ship underway at low water. When we got up at 8 o'clock the weather much pleasanter &amp; continued fine all day. At noon brought up at high water abreast of Whitaker Beason. Wind SW. At 5 pm,  low water, we again got under way, but were much delayed by a ship giving us a foul birth, the consequence was it became dark before we could work through Shoe hole  &amp; see the mouse buoy, where unfortunately it also turned very squally &amp; blowing a gale of wind. We were working towards the nose light &amp; should have got well up but the overhaul of our gib giving way the ship would not steer &amp; we were obliged, though not unwilling by ...</p>","pageId":769,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":629,"lock":null}
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/26/668
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":668,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":26,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... to bring up, being on good riding ground between the nose light &amp; the buoy of the mouse.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 11 o'clock we got into bed. Blowing a gale &amp; a dismal night</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":630,"lock":null}
20:13:19.138 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/26/668
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":668,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":26,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... to bring up, being on good riding ground between the nose light &amp; the buoy of the mouse.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 11 o'clock we got into bed. Blowing a gale &amp; a dismal night</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":630,"lock":null}
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/27/669
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":669,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":27,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out between 6 &amp; 7 o'clock &amp; soon afterward got underway. Still blowing hard from about the WSW lasting at 12 o'clock &amp; after it blew a perfect hurricane, however we rode well &amp; I slept very sound. A large Garth that we saw to leeward of us yesterday without his main topsail, is this morning upon the Maplin sand, having no doubt missed stage last night. Several smacks near him. This <span class=highlight_yellow>lide</span> we worked up as high as Southend &amp; brought up glad to get into such a good roadstead. At 6 o'clock, low water, the gale heavier &amp; night coming on, we did not think it prudent to get under way. At 8 o'clock we had a warm glass of Hollands &amp; water which we enjoyed. The first we have had for some time, having run out of coals.</p>\r\n<p>I forgot to mention that yesterday while we brought up abreast of the Whitaker, we lowered our boat &amp; went on board the Eliza of South Shields, Captain Young who kindly supplied us with two or three baskets of coals &amp; a cask of water in return we gave him a spare for a topgallant studding sail boom in dial plank &amp; a bottle of Hollands.</p>\r\n<p>He also took a box of Russian slippers &amp; a keg of caviar, to return to us in London. We fearing the officers might seize them</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":631,"lock":null}
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/27/669
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":669,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":27,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out between 6 &amp; 7 o'clock &amp; soon afterward got underway. Still blowing hard from about the WSW lasting at 12 o'clock &amp; after it blew a perfect hurricane, however we rode well &amp; I slept very sound. A large Garth that we saw to leeward of us yesterday without his main topsail, is this morning upon the Maplin sand, having no doubt missed stage last night. Several smacks near him. This <span class=highlight_yellow>lide</span> we worked up as high as Southend &amp; brought up glad to get into such a good roadstead. At 6 o'clock, low water, the gale heavier &amp; night coming on, we did not think it prudent to get under way. At 8 o'clock we had a warm glass of Hollands &amp; water which we enjoyed. The first we have had for some time, having run out of coals.</p>\r\n<p>I forgot to mention that yesterday while we brought up abreast of the Whitaker, we lowered our boat &amp; went on board the Eliza of South Shields, Captain Young who kindly supplied us with two or three baskets of coals &amp; a cask of water in return we gave him a spare for a topgallant studding sail boom in dial plank &amp; a bottle of Hollands.</p>\r\n<p>He also took a box of Russian slippers &amp; a keg of caviar, to return to us in London. We fearing the officers might seize them</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":631,"lock":null}
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1829/8/28/2324
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2324,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":28,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... were compelled to give the ship the whole chain. Wind WNW &amp; cloudy. Dismal weather with rain. A light ship close by us drove &amp; another broke his chain &amp; sun down the river. Continued blowing hard all day with heavy showers of rain &amp; squalls &amp; towards evening the wind got to about N by W but the tide not answering. <span class=highlight_yellow>Till</span> near dark, we lay still, indeed it blew too hard to think of getting under way &amp; one or two vessels that weighed were soon obliged to bring up again</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2264,"lock":null}
20:13:19.139 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
