20:13:19.250 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":881,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very wet morning, wind SE Afterwards changed to SW &amp; blowing fresh with fine weather. Brought a large fleet down amongst them the Hope. Dixon &amp; his aid Tom Smith Mrs Yellowley &amp; Emma came down to attend the funeral which took place between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock. Captain Caudisson of the 80<sup>th</sup> Regiment kindly allowing the hearse and carriages to enter the castle ...</p>","pageId":804,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":833,"lock":null}
20:13:19.250 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/15/882
20:13:19.250 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":882,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... yard. Mrs Scott's remains placed in the vault above my father. Fanny Smith more violent than ever. Her conduct most disgusting &amp; indecent. Dixon Mrs B &amp; Mr Gallowley returned to Newcastle. Tom Smith accompanied</p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":834,"lock":null}
20:13:19.250 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/15/882
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":882,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... yard. Mrs Scott's remains placed in the vault above my father. Fanny Smith more violent than ever. Her conduct most disgusting &amp; indecent. Dixon Mrs B &amp; Mr Gallowley returned to Newcastle. Tom Smith accompanied</p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":834,"lock":null}
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/16/883
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":883,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning. Wind NW &amp; rather cold. Tom Smith, his mother &amp; Fanny left us for Foyston &amp; fortunately without any more unpleasantness. After they left us the weather changed &amp; we had a very stormy day, heavy showers of snow Was &amp; I had the servants up &amp; told them of our plans. Robert agreed to remain with me, also Leadbetter. Hutchison of course leaves us, but will stay in the house until she gets a situation or I wish her to leave. Stephenson has been at NCastle  but could obtain nothing for the Hope only 8/6 pecght offering. Ordered Him to unbind the sails. Gave him a draft upon Chapman for &pound;20 to pay wages. At home all the evening</p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":835,"lock":null}
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/16/883
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":883,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":16,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning. Wind NW &amp; rather cold. Tom Smith, his mother &amp; Fanny left us for Foyston &amp; fortunately without any more unpleasantness. After they left us the weather changed &amp; we had a very stormy day, heavy showers of snow Was &amp; I had the servants up &amp; told them of our plans. Robert agreed to remain with me, also Leadbetter. Hutchison of course leaves us, but will stay in the house until she gets a situation or I wish her to leave. Stephenson has been at NCastle  but could obtain nothing for the Hope only 8/6 pecght offering. Ordered Him to unbind the sails. Gave him a draft upon Chapman for &pound;20 to pay wages. At home all the evening</p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":835,"lock":null}
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/17/884
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":884,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull showery day. Wind fresh at NW. Engaged at home in the house with ABS. I gave him his note at 40 days for &pound;75.10/- <font color=#FF0000>Accepted &amp; returned </font><font color=#FF0000>Mrs</font><font color=#FF0000> </font><font color=#FF0000>Bulemer</font><font color=#FF0000> a bill</font> <font color=#FF0000>at</font><font color=#FF0000> 3 </font><font color=#FF0000>hou</font><font color=#FF0000>rs. Due 23 p</font><font color=#FF0000>ricing</font><font color=#FF0000> for Policy presumabl</font><font color=#FF0000>y</font> <font color=#FF0000>almost</font><font color=#FF0000> &pound;16.13.8</font> Dined at home. At 5 o'clock the owner &amp; Master of the Commerce with me about the damages received by him in the wold when the Hope came in contact, agreed to leave it to two gentlemen to decide with have to call in a this. At home all the evening </p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":836,"lock":null}
20:13:19.251 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/17/884
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":884,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":17,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A dull showery day. Wind fresh at NW. Engaged at home in the house with ABS. I gave him his note at 40 days for &pound;75.10/- <font color=#FF0000>Accepted &amp; returned </font><font color=#FF0000>Mrs</font><font color=#FF0000> </font><font color=#FF0000>Bulemer</font><font color=#FF0000> a bill</font> <font color=#FF0000>at</font><font color=#FF0000> 3 </font><font color=#FF0000>hou</font><font color=#FF0000>rs. Due 23 p</font><font color=#FF0000>ricing</font><font color=#FF0000> for Policy presumabl</font><font color=#FF0000>y</font> <font color=#FF0000>almost</font><font color=#FF0000> &pound;16.13.8</font> Dined at home. At 5 o'clock the owner &amp; Master of the Commerce with me about the damages received by him in the wold when the Hope came in contact, agreed to leave it to two gentlemen to decide with have to call in a this. At home all the evening </p>","pageId":805,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":836,"lock":null}
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/18/885
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":885,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very boisterous day. Blowing hard at N &amp; NNW but fine weather. Payed E Ried apprentice &frac14;  years ways &pound;1.8.9 &amp; also Jeaslop the balance of his &frac14; wages &pound;1.3.9 &amp; advanced him 16/3 = &pound;2 Nman &amp; Leng aged all the moral with Robert Taking due account of the wine in the cellar. Mary Carts called &amp; in consequence of Mr Wrights bad conduct to her with whose children she was governess, we asked her to come and stay with us, until she goes to her situation as governess to a school in Newcastle. Dined at home at 2 o'clock. Paid into chapmans WBS; note for &pound;75 Miss Emma &amp; I took a walk round by Cullercoats Sands &amp; came home to tea. At the whist club in the evening.</p>","pageId":806,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":837,"lock":null}
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/18/885
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":885,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":18,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very boisterous day. Blowing hard at N &amp; NNW but fine weather. Payed E Ried apprentice &frac14;  years ways &pound;1.8.9 &amp; also Jeaslop the balance of his &frac14; wages &pound;1.3.9 &amp; advanced him 16/3 = &pound;2 Nman &amp; Leng aged all the moral with Robert Taking due account of the wine in the cellar. Mary Carts called &amp; in consequence of Mr Wrights bad conduct to her with whose children she was governess, we asked her to come and stay with us, until she goes to her situation as governess to a school in Newcastle. Dined at home at 2 o'clock. Paid into chapmans WBS; note for &pound;75 Miss Emma &amp; I took a walk round by Cullercoats Sands &amp; came home to tea. At the whist club in the evening.</p>","pageId":806,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":837,"lock":null}
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/dates/1830/3/19/886
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":886,"version":0,"year":1830,"month":3,"day":19,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Another boisterous day blowing a hurricane at about W by N Walked up to the Hope. Dined at home. After dinner went to the George to settle the damage done to the Commerce of Whitby referred it to A French &amp; H Walker who awarded the owner of the Commerce (Mr Wood) &pound;4 which I paid him. After which we had A few bottles of port. At the Mulstrum house in the Evening</p>","pageId":806,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":838,"lock":null}
20:13:19.252 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
