20:13:21.905 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2300,"version":8,"year":1829,"month":1,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p><span class=highlight_yellow>Called</span> upon Chapmans for &pound;10 for self &amp; gave Stephenson a draft for &pound;26.18.5 being the balance of his account for the last voyage. Paid into Chapmans WBS balance not foe &pound;30.5/-. Paid entrance &amp; expenses up to the end of the year to the whist club &pound;1.13/-</p>","pageId":720,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2240,"lock":null}
20:13:21.905 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2301
20:13:21.905 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2301,"version":4,"year":1829,"month":4,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>Could not attend the oyster supplier at the George in consequence. Gave Goe Slops 20/- on account of his wages.</p>","pageId":730,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2241,"lock":null}
20:13:21.905 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2301
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2301,"version":4,"year":1829,"month":4,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>Could not attend the oyster supplier at the George in consequence. Gave Goe Slops 20/- on account of his wages.</p>","pageId":730,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2241,"lock":null}
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2302
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2302,"version":2,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... in the morning. Packed my cloths up. H Harry &amp; I walked to Tynemouth in the evening &amp; took some gin punch with <span class=highlight_yellow>Daises</span>. Took leave of Mrs Scott &amp; slept on board the Hope. Our <span class=highlight_yellow>from</span><span class=highlight_yellow> all</span> passengers. Anne Stoke also on board.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2242,"lock":null}
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2302
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2302,"version":2,"year":1829,"month":5,"day":17,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... in the morning. Packed my cloths up. H Harry &amp; I walked to Tynemouth in the evening &amp; took some gin punch with <span class=highlight_yellow>Daises</span>. Took leave of Mrs Scott &amp; slept on board the Hope. Our <span class=highlight_yellow>from</span><span class=highlight_yellow> all</span> passengers. Anne Stoke also on board.</p>","pageId":734,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2242,"lock":null}
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2303
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2303,"version":6,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Petersburg&nbsp;voyage&nbsp;commences</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2243,"lock":null}
20:13:21.906 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2303
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2303,"version":6,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Petersburg&nbsp;voyage&nbsp;commences</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2243,"lock":null}
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2304
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2304,"version":3,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":19,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... we did see of the town, we were much pleased with. The houses are good and exceedingly neat &amp; clean inside &amp; out &amp; the inhabitants appeared to be a very respectable orderly set of people.</p>\r\n<p>Bob, JnoG <font color=#BFBFBF>[John G</font><font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>l</font><font color=#BFBFBF>braith] </font>&amp; I took a stroll even in the rain &amp; found our way into the church, where we remained some time. There was a large assemblage of children who were undergoing some examination by their chargeman. He appeared to be <span class=highlight_yellow>ca</span><span class=highlight_yellow>j</span><span class=highlight_yellow>oling</span> them, hard a great deal, but undeserved not a word that we saw, so came away as wise as we went. The alter was very splendidly ornamented &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>the</span> whole had a very grand imposing effect.</p>\r\n<p>The Captain was employed during the morning purchasing such stores as the ship required.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 1 o'clock pm we sat down to dinner &amp; were very hospitably entertained by W Chapman &amp; his father, too much so could, for we got rather too much wine &amp; I found myself pretty well <span class=highlight_yellow>s</span><span class=highlight_yellow>crew</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ed</span> by the time (5 or 6 o'clock) we had to go on bord again.</p>\r\n<p>Reached the ship at about &frac12; past 5 o'clock taking our pilot off with  us with the intention of getting under way &amp; running down to Copenhagen road, but it <font color=#BFBFBF>[was</font><font color=#BFBFBF>]</font> blowing a gale of wind, as it had been all day. &amp; having a foul birth from the number of ships. Lying about us, we could not get our anchor excepting at the risk of doing damage, so we determined to lay still until the wind abated.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock turned in for the night.</p>","pageId":741,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2244,"lock":null}
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2304
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2304,"version":3,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":19,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... we did see of the town, we were much pleased with. The houses are good and exceedingly neat &amp; clean inside &amp; out &amp; the inhabitants appeared to be a very respectable orderly set of people.</p>\r\n<p>Bob, JnoG <font color=#BFBFBF>[John G</font><font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>l</font><font color=#BFBFBF>braith] </font>&amp; I took a stroll even in the rain &amp; found our way into the church, where we remained some time. There was a large assemblage of children who were undergoing some examination by their chargeman. He appeared to be <span class=highlight_yellow>ca</span><span class=highlight_yellow>j</span><span class=highlight_yellow>oling</span> them, hard a great deal, but undeserved not a word that we saw, so came away as wise as we went. The alter was very splendidly ornamented &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>the</span> whole had a very grand imposing effect.</p>\r\n<p>The Captain was employed during the morning purchasing such stores as the ship required.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 1 o'clock pm we sat down to dinner &amp; were very hospitably entertained by W Chapman &amp; his father, too much so could, for we got rather too much wine &amp; I found myself pretty well <span class=highlight_yellow>s</span><span class=highlight_yellow>crew</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ed</span> by the time (5 or 6 o'clock) we had to go on bord again.</p>\r\n<p>Reached the ship at about &frac12; past 5 o'clock taking our pilot off with  us with the intention of getting under way &amp; running down to Copenhagen road, but it <font color=#BFBFBF>[was</font><font color=#BFBFBF>]</font> blowing a gale of wind, as it had been all day. &amp; having a foul birth from the number of ships. Lying about us, we could not get our anchor excepting at the risk of doing damage, so we determined to lay still until the wind abated.</p>\r\n<p>Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock turned in for the night.</p>","pageId":741,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2244,"lock":null}
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2305
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2305,"version":5,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... sails set. Perfectly astonished at the exceeding beauty of the coast, so finely wooded &amp; rich. &lsquo;tis impossible to imagine scenery more splendid.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 6 o'clock am came to an anchor in Copenhagen roads about &frac34; of a mile from the entrance to the harbour. Dressed ourselves &amp; got on shore about &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The mouth of the harbour is protected by a story battery (an island) called the Crown Battery, on both sides of which vessels can pass in, but on that side next to  <span class=highlight_yellow>Elscaeur</span> is the deepest water. We were obliged to stop at a platform where the <span class=highlight_yellow>Zuarantan</span> Officer is on the right hand side after passing the battery, where after being asked several questions, we were given a pass without which we should not have been allowed to land. The expense of this is one dollar for each person on board. Equal to about 2/3 a <span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ice</span>.</p>\r\n<p>Upon landing, we were <span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ssisted</span> by a person who proved to be <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman's agent at Copenhagen.  He undertook to do what was necessary for us, such as paying the <span class=highlight_yellow>dices</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> &amp; sent a person who accompanied us to their counting house. The firm, Heeksher, Peterson &amp; M&ouml;ller from whom we met with every attention.</p>\r\n<p>Called upon Mr Ryan, a personal friend of <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman, who had given us a letter of introduction to him. From him we also met with much <span class=highlight_yellow>pontificating</span>. At 8 o'clock went to the Hotel D'Angleterre &amp; had our breakfast &amp; afterwards proceeded, accompanied by a clerk of Mr Ryan, a very intelligent young Dame who talked English fluently. Arrived <span class=highlight_yellow>at</span> the town by the ramparts. As our time would not allow us to see much more than the town itself, but with it we were as touched &amp; delighted without exception. Taking it all together, it is the most beautiful city I ever saw. Most of cities have their S<sup>t</sup> Gales Bellengales &amp; but here there does not appear to be anything of the sort. &lsquo;tis all alike most ...</p>","pageId":742,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2245,"lock":null}
20:13:21.907 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2305
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2305,"version":5,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... sails set. Perfectly astonished at the exceeding beauty of the coast, so finely wooded &amp; rich. &lsquo;tis impossible to imagine scenery more splendid.</p>\r\n<p>At &frac12; past 6 o'clock am came to an anchor in Copenhagen roads about &frac34; of a mile from the entrance to the harbour. Dressed ourselves &amp; got on shore about &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The mouth of the harbour is protected by a story battery (an island) called the Crown Battery, on both sides of which vessels can pass in, but on that side next to  <span class=highlight_yellow>Elscaeur</span> is the deepest water. We were obliged to stop at a platform where the <span class=highlight_yellow>Zuarantan</span> Officer is on the right hand side after passing the battery, where after being asked several questions, we were given a pass without which we should not have been allowed to land. The expense of this is one dollar for each person on board. Equal to about 2/3 a <span class=highlight_yellow>p</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ice</span>.</p>\r\n<p>Upon landing, we were <span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ssisted</span> by a person who proved to be <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman's agent at Copenhagen.  He undertook to do what was necessary for us, such as paying the <span class=highlight_yellow>dices</span> <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> &amp; sent a person who accompanied us to their counting house. The firm, Heeksher, Peterson &amp; M&ouml;ller from whom we met with every attention.</p>\r\n<p>Called upon Mr Ryan, a personal friend of <span class=highlight_yellow>Mr</span> Chapman, who had given us a letter of introduction to him. From him we also met with much <span class=highlight_yellow>pontificating</span>. At 8 o'clock went to the Hotel D'Angleterre &amp; had our breakfast &amp; afterwards proceeded, accompanied by a clerk of Mr Ryan, a very intelligent young Dame who talked English fluently. Arrived <span class=highlight_yellow>at</span> the town by the ramparts. As our time would not allow us to see much more than the town itself, but with it we were as touched &amp; delighted without exception. Taking it all together, it is the most beautiful city I ever saw. Most of cities have their S<sup>t</sup> Gales Bellengales &amp; but here there does not appear to be anything of the sort. &lsquo;tis all alike most ...</p>","pageId":742,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2245,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2306
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2306,"version":7,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock &amp; most delightful morning, with a fine breeze from NW. been going 5 &amp; 6 knots all night. After breakfast going 6 knots &amp; steering an ENE course. At few of our companions, the dragon flies still with us. At 12 o'clock took observation &amp; by it, <span class=highlight_yellow>with</span> in lat:56.36. long: 18.41</p>","pageId":744,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2246,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2306
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2306,"version":7,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock &amp; most delightful morning, with a fine breeze from NW. been going 5 &amp; 6 knots all night. After breakfast going 6 knots &amp; steering an ENE course. At few of our companions, the dragon flies still with us. At 12 o'clock took observation &amp; by it, <span class=highlight_yellow>with</span> in lat:56.36. long: 18.41</p>","pageId":744,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2246,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2307
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2307,"version":2,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":29,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... debt. I should also mention that before the ship got to the mole, party of customs house officers came on board &amp; immediately scaled down all the hatches&amp; everything on board in the shape of a trunk or parcel belonging to us as passengers. The masters &amp; crew's trunks &amp; cabin lockers &amp; draws being <span class=highlight_yellow>examined</span>, the seals to <span class=highlight_yellow>re</span><span class=highlight_yellow>m</span><span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ce</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span> upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>karabis</span> until the collector came on board to search the ship.</p>\r\n<p>In the evening we walked about the town which is perfectly good for the plaice &amp; the fort exceedingly strong being covered with cannons &amp; having a garrison. I know not of how many men. I should think not numerous. The mole where the merchants vessels lay is a fine reservoir or wet dock capable of containing several hundred ships &amp; water enough for them to take in the whole of their cargos. There is also another mole for the men-of-war. We strolled about the town the whole of the evening &amp; when we looked at our watches much to our astonishment we found at nearly 12 o'clock  &amp; quite day light. In fact it is light all night here in summer. We returned to the ship, had our glass of spirit &amp; water &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2247,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2307
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2307,"version":2,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":29,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... debt. I should also mention that before the ship got to the mole, party of customs house officers came on board &amp; immediately scaled down all the hatches&amp; everything on board in the shape of a trunk or parcel belonging to us as passengers. The masters &amp; crew's trunks &amp; cabin lockers &amp; draws being <span class=highlight_yellow>examined</span>, the seals to <span class=highlight_yellow>re</span><span class=highlight_yellow>m</span><span class=highlight_yellow>a</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ce</span><span class=highlight_yellow>r</span> upon the <span class=highlight_yellow>karabis</span> until the collector came on board to search the ship.</p>\r\n<p>In the evening we walked about the town which is perfectly good for the plaice &amp; the fort exceedingly strong being covered with cannons &amp; having a garrison. I know not of how many men. I should think not numerous. The mole where the merchants vessels lay is a fine reservoir or wet dock capable of containing several hundred ships &amp; water enough for them to take in the whole of their cargos. There is also another mole for the men-of-war. We strolled about the town the whole of the evening &amp; when we looked at our watches much to our astonishment we found at nearly 12 o'clock  &amp; quite day light. In fact it is light all night here in summer. We returned to the ship, had our glass of spirit &amp; water &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":746,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2247,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2308
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2308,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... some prayer. Returned to the ship about 11 o'clock. Had a glass of cool brandy &amp; water &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2248,"lock":null}
20:13:21.908 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2308
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2308,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":30,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... some prayer. Returned to the ship about 11 o'clock. Had a glass of cool brandy &amp; water &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":747,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2248,"lock":null}
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2309
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2309,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... trip. On board we met Mr Fordeson who was also going up. Our papers were taken from us by an officer, who it appeared gave it to the master of the boat &amp; his duty I understand is to deliver them to some officer in Petersburg. We had a very pleasant passage <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of three houses &amp; landed about a mile below the city at the wharf of a Mr ____ a Scotchman &amp; proprietor of the steam boats. He was the first who started steam boats in Russia under an agreement with the Government that for a certain number of years no other boats were to be allowed to oppose him. He has thus accomplished a large fortune.</p>\r\n<p>Taking a <span class=highlight_yellow>donkey &amp; cart</span> for our luggage we drove to a Mrs Welson, a widow who keeps a boarding house in that part of the town called Backline. Here we found <font color=#BFBFBF>a </font>room &amp; after lunching, Bob &amp; I strolled out to endeavour &amp; find out the gentleman to whom we had letters of  introduction. The first person we called upon was a Mr Dye, a friend of In<font color=#BFBFBF>spect</font>o<font color=#BFBFBF>r</font>  Hodgson whom we found a very pleasant gentlemanly fellow residing in the first line, upon the Island &amp; in very elegant quarters. He gave us an invitation to dinner for tomorrow.</p>\r\n<p>We were struck with the magnificence of everything we saw. It not possible to summarize the splendour of the streets &amp; various buildings you see on every side as you pass along every house is upon a large &amp; noble scale &amp; whichever way your turn your eyes ...</p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2249,"lock":null}
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2309
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2309,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":2,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... trip. On board we met Mr Fordeson who was also going up. Our papers were taken from us by an officer, who it appeared gave it to the master of the boat &amp; his duty I understand is to deliver them to some officer in Petersburg. We had a very pleasant passage <span class=highlight_yellow>aft</span> of three houses &amp; landed about a mile below the city at the wharf of a Mr ____ a Scotchman &amp; proprietor of the steam boats. He was the first who started steam boats in Russia under an agreement with the Government that for a certain number of years no other boats were to be allowed to oppose him. He has thus accomplished a large fortune.</p>\r\n<p>Taking a <span class=highlight_yellow>donkey &amp; cart</span> for our luggage we drove to a Mrs Welson, a widow who keeps a boarding house in that part of the town called Backline. Here we found <font color=#BFBFBF>a </font>room &amp; after lunching, Bob &amp; I strolled out to endeavour &amp; find out the gentleman to whom we had letters of  introduction. The first person we called upon was a Mr Dye, a friend of In<font color=#BFBFBF>spect</font>o<font color=#BFBFBF>r</font>  Hodgson whom we found a very pleasant gentlemanly fellow residing in the first line, upon the Island &amp; in very elegant quarters. He gave us an invitation to dinner for tomorrow.</p>\r\n<p>We were struck with the magnificence of everything we saw. It not possible to summarize the splendour of the streets &amp; various buildings you see on every side as you pass along every house is upon a large &amp; noble scale &amp; whichever way your turn your eyes ...</p>","pageId":748,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2249,"lock":null}
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/231
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":231,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning. Wind SE Called &amp; paid Spence &amp; Foster for the main mast &pound;20.13/-. Walked to Tynemouth. A heavy thunder shower in the afternoon. Dined with Mrs Scott</p>","pageId":704,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":220,"lock":null}
20:13:21.909 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
