20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2312,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":7,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... city of Petersburg, it contains only three very small rooms, something after this plan <font color=#00B0F0>[See figure]</font>  Here is also to be see the boat which he built himself. How it is <span class=highlight_yellow>made</span> known he was an excellent carpenter &amp; used to row in. In the Citadel church there are some beautiful things in ivory of his turning &amp; workmanship.</p>\r\n<p>Dined with Mr Gillibrand only his wife &amp; Mr Lindsey there. Spent a very quiet day &amp; left early being foreign port day, which over the water &amp; took tea with a friend of Mr Lumleys &amp; Bob &amp; I afterwards had a stroll down the Mews by Prospect to see the people &amp; illuminations for the Emperors birthday. They don't as with us place candle &amp; light in the windows etc, but <span class=highlight_yellow>feet</span> what they call <span class=highlight_yellow>fat pots</span> along the street upon the edge of the pavement &amp; most horrible <span class=highlight_yellow>effluence</span> they send forth. We got to bed about 12 o'clock</p>","pageId":752,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2252,"lock":null}
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2313
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2313,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... the <span class=highlight_yellow>Pete</span><span class=highlight_yellow>rs</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ho</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ff</span> Road, but being plagued with my toothache &amp; my jaw much swollen, I was obliged to let Bob go by himself &amp; dined at the club with Mr Lindsey. He &amp; I in the evening each got a horse of Me Webster who keeps excellent cattle &amp; took a very pleasant ride down by <span class=highlight_yellow>Catharinoff</span> &amp; round home by the <span class=highlight_yellow>Petershoff</span> Road. After having our grog, retired to bed between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock, exceedingly stiff &amp; fatigued.</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2253,"lock":null}
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2313
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2313,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":8,"sequence":6.0000,"text":"<p>... the <span class=highlight_yellow>Pete</span><span class=highlight_yellow>rs</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ho</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ff</span> Road, but being plagued with my toothache &amp; my jaw much swollen, I was obliged to let Bob go by himself &amp; dined at the club with Mr Lindsey. He &amp; I in the evening each got a horse of Me Webster who keeps excellent cattle &amp; took a very pleasant ride down by <span class=highlight_yellow>Catharinoff</span> &amp; round home by the <span class=highlight_yellow>Petershoff</span> Road. After having our grog, retired to bed between 11 &amp; 12 o'clock, exceedingly stiff &amp; fatigued.</p>","pageId":753,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2253,"lock":null}
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2314
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
20:13:21.911 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2314
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2314,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":9,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... intended for the foreign ambassadors when the court is there.</p>\r\n<p>In the grounds we took lunch &amp; at the same time had the pleasure of being most unmercifully bit by mosquitoes.</p>\r\n<p>From this we drove to Parlousky another Palace the residence of the late <span class=highlight_yellow>Dowagn</span> Empress the widow of Paul &amp; mother of the present Emperor. It is a handsome building but nothing splendid in its appearance. The interior we did not see. The grounds though not as extensive, I think more beautiful than Jarskoi. There is more variety of scenery, hill &amp; dale nature has dome much for it. The other is <span class=highlight_yellow>obliged</span> to ask. In these grounds are some very fine statues &amp; near the Palace is one of the most splendidly executed things I ever saw, these female figures upon a pedestal in marble as large as life, <span class=highlight_yellow>supposing</span> an <span class=highlight_yellow>usn</span> a <span class=highlight_yellow>ne</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>plas</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>lettra</span><span class=highlight_yellow> of statuary</span>.</p>\r\n<p>After taking some brandy &amp; water at the top of the waterfall to quench our thirst, we again got into the carriage &amp; drove homewards, stopping at a fine fountain on the road where we dined, having carried what <span class=highlight_yellow>prog</span> &amp; drinkables we wanted with us.</p>\r\n<p>When at Jarskoi we saw the Emperor's cows, pastures and fine animals &amp; the breeds of all nations. This establishment the late Emperor Alexander took great delight in, and it is kept up at considerable extent. The cows are dressed the same as horses &amp; in fine condition.</p>","pageId":754,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2254,"lock":null}
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2315
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2315
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2315,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":11,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... took a walk in the summer gardens of the Palace. On our return had a glass of grog with Mr Lindsey &amp; got to bed at 12 o'clock.</p>\r\n<p>Got admission into the premises where they are building the new Isaac Church &amp; now engaged in erecting the granite pillars for the Portico &amp; they use of an immense size most beautifully polished. The finest &amp; longest in the world, brought from the quarries in Finland, their circumference about 30 feet &amp; height upwards of 50 &amp; although they weight ___ tons, they raise them in 20 minutes</p>","pageId":755,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2255,"lock":null}
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2316
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2316,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... filled with ancient armour, arms &amp; belonging to various nations and exceedingly curious, colours &amp; standards taken battle, models of fortifications, cannon &amp; mortars, the cloths, arms &amp; accoutrements belonging to peter the Great, the late Emperor Alexander &amp; several Generals etc. etc. etc. all very curious &amp; well worth seeing, particularly the funeral car for the late Alexander, a most magnificent vehicle.</p>\r\n<p>After leaving the Arsenal Me Lindsley accompanied us to the market near the Cesan Church where we purchased several articles as presents for the Lees etc.</p>\r\n<p>Came home to dinner, Mr Lindsley with us had <span class=highlight_yellow>Sadaek</span> for dinner, <span class=highlight_yellow>these</span> fish and another called <span class=highlight_yellow>Saggee</span> are fine flavoured &amp; most excellent.</p>\r\n<p>The pay of the Russian soldiers &amp; officers is very trifling. That of a <span class=highlight_yellow>S</span><span class=highlight_yellow>aba</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ll</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ere</span> &pound;16 a year &amp; a private 11 Rubbles, not much more than 9/- English. The private of the bodyguard have 13 Rubbles</p>\r\n<p>Being rather tired in the evening we only took a short walk down the Newsky Prospect</p>","pageId":757,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2256,"lock":null}
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2316
20:13:21.912 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2316,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... filled with ancient armour, arms &amp; belonging to various nations and exceedingly curious, colours &amp; standards taken battle, models of fortifications, cannon &amp; mortars, the cloths, arms &amp; accoutrements belonging to peter the Great, the late Emperor Alexander &amp; several Generals etc. etc. etc. all very curious &amp; well worth seeing, particularly the funeral car for the late Alexander, a most magnificent vehicle.</p>\r\n<p>After leaving the Arsenal Me Lindsley accompanied us to the market near the Cesan Church where we purchased several articles as presents for the Lees etc.</p>\r\n<p>Came home to dinner, Mr Lindsley with us had <span class=highlight_yellow>Sadaek</span> for dinner, <span class=highlight_yellow>these</span> fish and another called <span class=highlight_yellow>Saggee</span> are fine flavoured &amp; most excellent.</p>\r\n<p>The pay of the Russian soldiers &amp; officers is very trifling. That of a <span class=highlight_yellow>S</span><span class=highlight_yellow>aba</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ll</span><span class=highlight_yellow>ere</span> &pound;16 a year &amp; a private 11 Rubbles, not much more than 9/- English. The private of the bodyguard have 13 Rubbles</p>\r\n<p>Being rather tired in the evening we only took a short walk down the Newsky Prospect</p>","pageId":757,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2256,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2317
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2317,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. in the vapour bath, which we looked into, but that was all for it was, so intolerably hot  we were glad to draw back, others afterwards having part oiled themselves &amp; perspiring most copiously at every pore were employed scouring &amp; pumping upon each other with cold water, all of course en buff. We next took the liberty of paying the ladies a visit, whom we saw the same way employed &amp; in the same state of nudity. Their moderately poor <span class=highlight_yellow>innocents</span> did not make them shrink at all from our scrutinising gaze. Indeed they seemed to enjoy the fun as much as we did. I think there were from 30 to 40 of them. We didist not venture to look into the ladies vapour bath, for we could not get to without passing through ducking <span class=highlight_yellow>guard</span> &amp; we were afraid they might favour us with a sousing.</p>\r\n<p>We then proceeded to the market &amp; purchased several pairs of slippers etc &amp; returned home by the summer gardens. Took a couple of glasses of iced grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":758,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2257,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2317
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2317,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":16,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. in the vapour bath, which we looked into, but that was all for it was, so intolerably hot  we were glad to draw back, others afterwards having part oiled themselves &amp; perspiring most copiously at every pore were employed scouring &amp; pumping upon each other with cold water, all of course en buff. We next took the liberty of paying the ladies a visit, whom we saw the same way employed &amp; in the same state of nudity. Their moderately poor <span class=highlight_yellow>innocents</span> did not make them shrink at all from our scrutinising gaze. Indeed they seemed to enjoy the fun as much as we did. I think there were from 30 to 40 of them. We didist not venture to look into the ladies vapour bath, for we could not get to without passing through ducking <span class=highlight_yellow>guard</span> &amp; we were afraid they might favour us with a sousing.</p>\r\n<p>We then proceeded to the market &amp; purchased several pairs of slippers etc &amp; returned home by the summer gardens. Took a couple of glasses of iced grog &amp; went to bed</p>","pageId":758,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2257,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2318
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2318,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... humour to go on shore. Obliged to go to bed. The pain &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>opium</span> making me exceptionally drowsy. Bought some flannel &amp; hartshorn in the evening which I put upon my face when I went to bed</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2258,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2318
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2318,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... humour to go on shore. Obliged to go to bed. The pain &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>opium</span> making me exceptionally drowsy. Bought some flannel &amp; hartshorn in the evening which I put upon my face when I went to bed</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2258,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2319
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2319,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... not inhabited. They are all within a stone's throw of each other. After passing Ertholmene, we had a most delightful sail under the Island of Bornholm &amp; a good view of it. This is one of the richest and most beautiful islands that can be imagined. It is in fact a perfect garden &amp; to us had all the appearance of a gentleman's park. The soil is good &amp; exceedingly productive yielding as much in one season as would keep the inhabitants several years. A boat came out to us from the shore &amp; we purchased of them four ducks &frac34; score of eggs &amp; 2 pounds of butter for which we gave only 6/6</p>\r\n<p>When we reached the west end of the island then wind took us all at a <span class=highlight_yellow>lk</span>, but soon favoured us again. In the evening it became quite calm. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock the captain, Bob &amp; I as usual had our grog &amp; being in the humour, <span class=highlight_yellow>had</span> some supper &amp; had a regular jollification, blew a cloud &amp; got to roost between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2259,"lock":null}
20:13:21.913 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2319
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2319,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":5,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... not inhabited. They are all within a stone's throw of each other. After passing Ertholmene, we had a most delightful sail under the Island of Bornholm &amp; a good view of it. This is one of the richest and most beautiful islands that can be imagined. It is in fact a perfect garden &amp; to us had all the appearance of a gentleman's park. The soil is good &amp; exceedingly productive yielding as much in one season as would keep the inhabitants several years. A boat came out to us from the shore &amp; we purchased of them four ducks &frac34; score of eggs &amp; 2 pounds of butter for which we gave only 6/6</p>\r\n<p>When we reached the west end of the island then wind took us all at a <span class=highlight_yellow>lk</span>, but soon favoured us again. In the evening it became quite calm. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock the captain, Bob &amp; I as usual had our grog &amp; being in the humour, <span class=highlight_yellow>had</span> some supper &amp; had a regular jollification, blew a cloud &amp; got to roost between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock</p>","pageId":763,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2259,"lock":null}
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/232
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":232,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":8,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very fine day. Wind SE Engaged in the office with Robert. Walked to Tynemouth &amp; took a warm bath. Dined with Mrs Scott. Walked in the evening to Carr's Wall Spout The Hope got under it in the morning &amp; to come off at night with 13 or 14 Keels. The remainder to follow</p>","pageId":704,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":221,"lock":null}
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/232
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":232,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":8,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A very fine day. Wind SE Engaged in the office with Robert. Walked to Tynemouth &amp; took a warm bath. Dined with Mrs Scott. Walked in the evening to Carr's Wall Spout The Hope got under it in the morning &amp; to come off at night with 13 or 14 Keels. The remainder to follow</p>","pageId":704,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":221,"lock":null}
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2320
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2320,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. glass of wine with him &amp; I don't know how to account for it, excepting it was drinking upon an empty stomach but I really got muzzy of which I was sensible enough to feel heartily ashamed when I had to appear in the presence of Mr ships Chapmans. Got on board a little after 12 o'clock after one or two adventured before we left the town H French's ship, the 3 Brothers &amp; several other vessels passed us upward bound between Copenhagen &amp; Elsinore</p>","pageId":764,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2260,"lock":null}
20:13:21.914 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.915 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2320
20:13:21.915 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2320,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>.. glass of wine with him &amp; I don't know how to account for it, excepting it was drinking upon an empty stomach but I really got muzzy of which I was sensible enough to feel heartily ashamed when I had to appear in the presence of Mr ships Chapmans. Got on board a little after 12 o'clock after one or two adventured before we left the town H French's ship, the 3 Brothers &amp; several other vessels passed us upward bound between Copenhagen &amp; Elsinore</p>","pageId":764,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2260,"lock":null}
20:13:21.915 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2321
20:13:21.915 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2321,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... the stateroom scuttle light, wet my bed &amp; I did not expect to have had one to sleep in tonight, indeed had the gale continued I should not have cared about it, but I contrived by placing canvas between the bed and sides of the vessel to fortify it pretty well from the wet &amp; thus gained a better nights rest than I anticipated.</p>\r\n<p>My face beginning to swell again, but no pain.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2261,"lock":null}
20:13:21.915 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2321
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2321,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":15,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... the stateroom scuttle light, wet my bed &amp; I did not expect to have had one to sleep in tonight, indeed had the gale continued I should not have cared about it, but I contrived by placing canvas between the bed and sides of the vessel to fortify it pretty well from the wet &amp; thus gained a better nights rest than I anticipated.</p>\r\n<p>My face beginning to swell again, but no pain.</p>","pageId":766,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2261,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2322
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2322,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... get at them. Bob &amp; I much annoyed having made certain of having a comfortable dinner &amp; bed on show &amp; talked over our plans of future proceedings. He having determined to stay a few days at Shields &amp; take his passage in some other vessel.</p>\r\n<p>The wind &amp; sea being right aft, we soon passed Huntcliff foot again &amp; at 8 o'clock left Flamborough Head bearing west distant 2 or 3 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great many light ships in Burlington Bat as we passed.</p>\r\n<p>Ship made a great deal of water during the day</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2262,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2322
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2322,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":20,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... get at them. Bob &amp; I much annoyed having made certain of having a comfortable dinner &amp; bed on show &amp; talked over our plans of future proceedings. He having determined to stay a few days at Shields &amp; take his passage in some other vessel.</p>\r\n<p>The wind &amp; sea being right aft, we soon passed Huntcliff foot again &amp; at 8 o'clock left Flamborough Head bearing west distant 2 or 3 miles.</p>\r\n<p>A great many light ships in Burlington Bat as we passed.</p>\r\n<p>Ship made a great deal of water during the day</p>","pageId":767,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2262,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2323
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2323
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2323,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":22,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... struggled hard to prevent it escaping. I felt that &quot;Richard was himself again&quot; &amp; no longer the prospect for the present of ruin &amp; a jail before me. At 4 o'clock pm we brought up abreast of the Navy Hospital, within a short distance of the shore, blowing a heavy gale from the SW &amp; rejoiced that we had got into such a snug roadstead, instead of being at sea at night</p>\r\n<p>Such tempestuous &amp; unsettled weather for the time of year I don't remember. This is the fourth gale we have experienced since leaving Elseneur &amp; although it is such a short distance to the river, I almost despair of reaching without some disaster for bad luck seems to thwart us, whichever way we steer.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I turned in a little after 8 o'clock. The gale rather encased with rain</p>","pageId":768,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2263,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2324
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2324,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":28,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... were compelled to give the ship the whole chain. Wind WNW &amp; cloudy. Dismal weather with rain. A light ship close by us drove &amp; another broke his chain &amp; sun down the river. Continued blowing hard all day with heavy showers of rain &amp; squalls &amp; towards evening the wind got to about N by W but the tide not answering. <span class=highlight_yellow>Till</span> near dark, we lay still, indeed it blew too hard to think of getting under way &amp; one or two vessels that weighed were soon obliged to bring up again</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2264,"lock":null}
20:13:21.916 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2324
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2324,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":8,"day":28,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... were compelled to give the ship the whole chain. Wind WNW &amp; cloudy. Dismal weather with rain. A light ship close by us drove &amp; another broke his chain &amp; sun down the river. Continued blowing hard all day with heavy showers of rain &amp; squalls &amp; towards evening the wind got to about N by W but the tide not answering. <span class=highlight_yellow>Till</span> near dark, we lay still, indeed it blew too hard to think of getting under way &amp; one or two vessels that weighed were soon obliged to bring up again</p>","pageId":770,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2264,"lock":null}
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2325
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2325,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":11,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... since I had the pleasure of seeing him. We afterwards took a Greenwich boat &amp; went down to the Hope upon Dowslands way where we dined off cold mutton. At low water looked at her bottom &amp; gave orders for her to go into the dock to be caulked. Turned out an exceptionally wet evening. Had our tea on board &amp; afterwards took a <span class=highlight_yellow>Mackware</span> stage to Leadenhall Street &amp; then walked to New Inn. Bob Marsh at the theatre.</p>","pageId":773,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2265,"lock":null}
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/2325
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":2325,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":11,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>... since I had the pleasure of seeing him. We afterwards took a Greenwich boat &amp; went down to the Hope upon Dowslands way where we dined off cold mutton. At low water looked at her bottom &amp; gave orders for her to go into the dock to be caulked. Turned out an exceptionally wet evening. Had our tea on board &amp; afterwards took a <span class=highlight_yellow>Mackware</span> stage to Leadenhall Street &amp; then walked to New Inn. Bob Marsh at the theatre.</p>","pageId":773,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2265,"lock":null}
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/2326
20:13:21.917 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":2326,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":9,"day":14,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>Rather showery. Called at the New Inn. Put my <font color=#222222>portmanteau</font>  &amp; bag into a cab &amp; drove up to Nottingham Place. Spent the evening quietly at home.</p>","pageId":774,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":2266,"lock":null}
