20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":548,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":5,"sequence":5.0000,"text":"<p>A continuance  of fine weather. Wind NW. Mr Lee drove me into the city. Walked down to the ship upon Dowland's Ways. The painters finished painting the cabin &amp; busy with the sides. Made a port hole into the state room. Moored the ship down in Duke Shore &amp; got ballast. Took boat to the tower &amp; walked from thence to  Mr Lees to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":526,"lock":null}
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/549
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":549,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning &amp; a fresh breeze from NW. Walked into the city with William. Got a new hat at Olipharts. Called for Bob Marsh &amp; we afterwards went by water to the Hope. Walked home to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":527,"lock":null}
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/549
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":549,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":6,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning &amp; a fresh breeze from NW. Walked into the city with William. Got a new hat at Olipharts. Called for Bob Marsh &amp; we afterwards went by water to the Hope. Walked home to dinner</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":527,"lock":null}
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/55
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":55,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":2,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind WSW. Got the Hope flats finished caulking &amp; blocks stowed. Brought a new mainmast of Miles Forster at &pound;7.10 per load less discount = &pound;20.11. Dined at home. At Mrs Clarks in evening a party</p>","pageId":690,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":52,"lock":null}
20:13:22.071 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/55
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":55,"version":0,"year":1828,"month":2,"day":20,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day. Wind WSW. Got the Hope flats finished caulking &amp; blocks stowed. Brought a new mainmast of Miles Forster at &pound;7.10 per load less discount = &pound;20.11. Dined at home. At Mrs Clarks in evening a party</p>","pageId":690,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":52,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/550
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":550,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day, but weather cooler. Wind NE. I went to the Roman Catholic Chapel in Warwick Street &amp; much pleased with the ringing. Went to the park, met Bob Marsh &amp; proceeded to Kensington Gardens. A great crowd of fashionable company there &amp; many beautiful women. Dined at Mr Lees.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":528,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/550
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":550,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":7,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine day, but weather cooler. Wind NE. I went to the Roman Catholic Chapel in Warwick Street &amp; much pleased with the ringing. Went to the park, met Bob Marsh &amp; proceeded to Kensington Gardens. A great crowd of fashionable company there &amp; many beautiful women. Dined at Mr Lees.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":528,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/551
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":551,"version":1,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Wind NW. A good deal of rain after a breakfast. Walked into the city. Brought a filtering machine for the ship. Gave Stephenson &pound;25.  Settled the freight account with William &amp; received the balance &pound;30.4/-. Called at the Russian consul for passports.  Dined at Richmonds. Hard J Jobling Chambers &amp; there. When I got home, found J Gilbraith had arrived.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":529,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/551
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":551,"version":1,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":8,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>A dull morning. Wind NW. A good deal of rain after a breakfast. Walked into the city. Brought a filtering machine for the ship. Gave Stephenson &pound;25.  Settled the freight account with William &amp; received the balance &pound;30.4/-. Called at the Russian consul for passports.  Dined at Richmonds. Hard J Jobling Chambers &amp; there. When I got home, found J Gilbraith had arrived.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":529,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/552
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":552,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but rather dull. Wind NNE. <span class=highlight_yellow>Later</span> J Galbraith &amp; I took a coach from Nottingham Place for the city with our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> the ship's boat with four hands at Billingsgate to carry all our things to the ship. <del>Called upon the Russian Consol.</del> The party with one Vinccent carrying &frac12; dozen wine with us took a waterman at the Customs House &amp; went down to the ship to dinner. In the evening attended Greenwich Fair which was not so crowded as I have seen it &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>letto</span><span class=highlight_yellow> run</span>. I called upon G Watts. William, Lee &amp; Vincent left us &amp; Bob Marsh J.G. &amp; I returned to the ship a little after 10 o'clock to sleep. Bob &amp; I smoked a cloud, wet both eyes &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":530,"lock":null}
20:13:22.072 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/552
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":552,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":9,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A fine morning, but rather dull. Wind NNE. <span class=highlight_yellow>Later</span> J Galbraith &amp; I took a coach from Nottingham Place for the city with our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>for</span> the ship's boat with four hands at Billingsgate to carry all our things to the ship. <del>Called upon the Russian Consol.</del> The party with one Vinccent carrying &frac12; dozen wine with us took a waterman at the Customs House &amp; went down to the ship to dinner. In the evening attended Greenwich Fair which was not so crowded as I have seen it &amp; <span class=highlight_yellow>letto</span><span class=highlight_yellow> run</span>. I called upon G Watts. William, Lee &amp; Vincent left us &amp; Bob Marsh J.G. &amp; I returned to the ship a little after 10 o'clock to sleep. Bob &amp; I smoked a cloud, wet both eyes &amp; turned in.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":530,"lock":null}
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/553
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":553,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. Turned out between 7 &amp; * o'clock am &amp; breakfasted. The ship under way with a foul wind at NNE. Bob Marsh, John G<font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>lb</font><font color=#BFBFBF>raith</font> &amp; I got a waterman &amp; returned to London to see William again &amp; get some of our cloths. Gave WBS a my note on <span class=highlight_yellow>detailed</span> for &pound;300 because <span class=highlight_yellow>int</span> I unfortunately being considerably in his debt. We all dined in Water lane at 3 o'clock pm &amp; at 4 o'clock embarked on board to the Hope, lying at Gallions. Had tea. Employed ourselves in the evening stowing all our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>&amp;</span> away &amp; getting things into something like shipshape. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> a couple of glasses of gin &amp; water &amp; about 11 o'clock turned in, feeling my cold rather worse. With a <span class=highlight_yellow>cough</span> at my chest. Wrote today to the Sadler declining to join him in the establishment of a paper manufactury at Shields.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":531,"lock":null}
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/553
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":553,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":10,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>A pleasant morning. Turned out between 7 &amp; * o'clock am &amp; breakfasted. The ship under way with a foul wind at NNE. Bob Marsh, John G<font color=#BFBFBF>a</font><font color=#BFBFBF>lb</font><font color=#BFBFBF>raith</font> &amp; I got a waterman &amp; returned to London to see William again &amp; get some of our cloths. Gave WBS a my note on <span class=highlight_yellow>detailed</span> for &pound;300 because <span class=highlight_yellow>int</span> I unfortunately being considerably in his debt. We all dined in Water lane at 3 o'clock pm &amp; at 4 o'clock embarked on board to the Hope, lying at Gallions. Had tea. Employed ourselves in the evening stowing all our cloths <span class=highlight_yellow>&amp;</span> away &amp; getting things into something like shipshape. Between 9 &amp; 10 o'clock blew a cloud, <span class=highlight_yellow>discharged</span> a couple of glasses of gin &amp; water &amp; about 11 o'clock turned in, feeling my cold rather worse. With a <span class=highlight_yellow>cough</span> at my chest. Wrote today to the Sadler declining to join him in the establishment of a paper manufactury at Shields.</p>","pageId":737,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":531,"lock":null}
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/554
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":554,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":11,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out a little after 7 o'clock am. Thick weather &amp; wind very variable between E &amp; NNW. At &frac12; past 9 o'clock got under way &amp; worked down with the tide. At &frac12; past 3 o'clock being low water, we brought up at Grays &amp; had our dinner. Several very fine sailing boats past us, being a race for a cup, in the following order as they returned after going round the boat at <span class=highlight_yellow>south</span> fleet, the <span class=highlight_yellow>Einnlation</span> , Rob Bery, Yela, Lady Luisa &amp; Royal Eagle.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob &amp; I <span class=highlight_yellow>discerfsed</span> a bottle of Madiera. At 7 o'clock had tea. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock being high water we again got under way &amp; worked down to Gravesend where we brought up for the night at 10 o'clock, smoked two pipes, finished the like number of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; at 11 o'clock went to roost. Wind very variable during the day between SE and NNW.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":532,"lock":null}
20:13:22.073 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/554
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":554,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":11,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out a little after 7 o'clock am. Thick weather &amp; wind very variable between E &amp; NNW. At &frac12; past 9 o'clock got under way &amp; worked down with the tide. At &frac12; past 3 o'clock being low water, we brought up at Grays &amp; had our dinner. Several very fine sailing boats past us, being a race for a cup, in the following order as they returned after going round the boat at <span class=highlight_yellow>south</span> fleet, the <span class=highlight_yellow>Einnlation</span> , Rob Bery, Yela, Lady Luisa &amp; Royal Eagle.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner, Bob &amp; I <span class=highlight_yellow>discerfsed</span> a bottle of Madiera. At 7 o'clock had tea. At &frac12; past 8 o'clock being high water we again got under way &amp; worked down to Gravesend where we brought up for the night at 10 o'clock, smoked two pipes, finished the like number of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; at 11 o'clock went to roost. Wind very variable during the day between SE and NNW.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":532,"lock":null}
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/555
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":555,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 7 o'clock. Every appearance of being a very after a thick foggy night. Wind about East. After breakfast Bob Marsh &amp; I went on shore to endeavour to get each a straw hat, but did not succeed. Returned to the ship again before 10 o'clock. During our absence they had got her under way. At low water having had a fresh breeze from the E to ESE. We brought up abreast of Canvey Island, off Leigh at &frac12; past 2 o'clock. Had our dinner which <span class=highlight_yellow>was</span> devoured with a pretty considerable appetite.  Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Took tea at 7 o'clock and at 9 o'clock again got underway with a light breeze <span class=highlight_yellow>from about</span> SSE which carried us down to Southend when  we let go our anchor for the night at &frac12; past 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Discharged</span> a couple of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; turned in at &frac14; to 12 o'clock. Quite ready for a good snooze.</p>\r\n<p>Should the wind continue from the eastward our intention is to go on shore &amp; spend the day at Southend.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":533,"lock":null}
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/555
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":555,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":12,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Got up at 7 o'clock. Every appearance of being a very after a thick foggy night. Wind about East. After breakfast Bob Marsh &amp; I went on shore to endeavour to get each a straw hat, but did not succeed. Returned to the ship again before 10 o'clock. During our absence they had got her under way. At low water having had a fresh breeze from the E to ESE. We brought up abreast of Canvey Island, off Leigh at &frac12; past 2 o'clock. Had our dinner which <span class=highlight_yellow>was</span> devoured with a pretty considerable appetite.  Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Took tea at 7 o'clock and at 9 o'clock again got underway with a light breeze <span class=highlight_yellow>from about</span> SSE which carried us down to Southend when  we let go our anchor for the night at &frac12; past 10 o'clock. <span class=highlight_yellow>Discharged</span> a couple of glasses of rum &amp; water &amp; turned in at &frac14; to 12 o'clock. Quite ready for a good snooze.</p>\r\n<p>Should the wind continue from the eastward our intention is to go on shore &amp; spend the day at Southend.</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":533,"lock":null}
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/556
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
20:13:22.074 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/556
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":556,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":13,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>I woke a little after 5 o'clock this morning by the crew preparing to get underway, the wind having changed to the westward, so that our intentions of going on shore at Southend has been frustrated. Turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. The ship not much below Southend there being scarcely enough wind to set us against the tide. A most beautiful morning &amp; exceedingly hot. Passed the Earl Balcarras Indiaman homeward bound. A very fine vessel  &amp; also a 74 foot ship at anchor above the <span class=highlight_yellow>Hove</span> Light. Several smaller vessels principally foreigners. Mr Has Venson <span class=highlight_yellow>proceed &amp;</span> <span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>be</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span><span class=highlight_yellow>d_d</span><span class=highlight_yellow> </span>to them. In company with us. A beautiful sight, the sea as smooth as oil. At 10 o'clock am driving with the tide, a dead calm. Fear we shall make but a had tide work. The wind seems much incined to get round to the eastward again. About 10 o'clock a little below the Nou Light, the wind took us all aback, changing to the ESE. After making a few tacks, we increased to a fine breeze &amp; we proceeded down <span class=highlight_yellow>wind</span> gradually increasing our rate to about 7 knots an hour. At &frac14; to 5 &amp; 6 o'clock pm. Passed the <span class=highlight_yellow>Suak</span> Light. We have had a most glorious day, exceptionally hot &amp; singing our <span class=highlight_yellow>phizogs</span> in grand style. Bob &amp; I again <span class=highlight_yellow>dispatched</span> a bottle of Maderia after dinner upon deck. If we go on at this rate, I reckon our stock of wine (originally only &frac12; dozen) will only last three days longer. Today we dined at a little after 12 o'clock sea hours. Employed during the morning in preparing our fishing gear. My cold worse this evening &amp; felt exceedingly chilly. Took a stiff glass of warm rum &amp; water &amp; went to bed. Set steering sails</p>","pageId":738,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":534,"lock":null}
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/557
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":557,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>During the night it has blown a steady breeze, fair for us &amp; we have gone along steadily &amp; pleasant at about the rate of 6 knots. After a good snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Not a cloud to be seen, with a find steady &amp; refreshing breeze from SW by W. Steady sails still continued set. After breakfast read the morning service &amp; lessons. Passed through a large fleet of Dutch fishing boats, many of them came near &amp; wished us much to buy some fish. Dined between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock &amp; Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Only one more left, which we have determined, if possible, to keep for next Sunday. We have not averaged this day more than 4 knots an hour.</p>\r\n<p>Started a ENE course. The master &amp; mate at 12 o'clock took their observation on latitude 53.8 North Long 3.30. Distance from the Sunk, run since 6 last evening 111 miles. Changed our course to NE.</p>\r\n<p>At 9 o'clock took our rum &amp; water &amp; afterwards being a most beautiful moonlit night, we walked the deck for &frac12; an hour &amp; at &frac12; past 10 o'clock turned in, at which time we had a better breeze &amp; had increased our rate to about 6 knots.</p>\r\n<p><span class=highlight_yellow>Robecia</span> Filtering Apparatus answered admirably yielding an ample supply of most beautiful clear &amp; pure water, from the filthy Thames mud</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":535,"lock":null}
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/557
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":557,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":14,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>During the night it has blown a steady breeze, fair for us &amp; we have gone along steadily &amp; pleasant at about the rate of 6 knots. After a good snooze, turned out at &frac12; past 7 o'clock. A most delightful morning. Not a cloud to be seen, with a find steady &amp; refreshing breeze from SW by W. Steady sails still continued set. After breakfast read the morning service &amp; lessons. Passed through a large fleet of Dutch fishing boats, many of them came near &amp; wished us much to buy some fish. Dined between 12 &amp; 1 o'clock &amp; Bob &amp; I punished another bottle of Madeira. Only one more left, which we have determined, if possible, to keep for next Sunday. We have not averaged this day more than 4 knots an hour.</p>\r\n<p>Started a ENE course. The master &amp; mate at 12 o'clock took their observation on latitude 53.8 North Long 3.30. Distance from the Sunk, run since 6 last evening 111 miles. Changed our course to NE.</p>\r\n<p>At 9 o'clock took our rum &amp; water &amp; afterwards being a most beautiful moonlit night, we walked the deck for &frac12; an hour &amp; at &frac12; past 10 o'clock turned in, at which time we had a better breeze &amp; had increased our rate to about 6 knots.</p>\r\n<p><span class=highlight_yellow>Robecia</span> Filtering Apparatus answered admirably yielding an ample supply of most beautiful clear &amp; pure water, from the filthy Thames mud</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":535,"lock":null}
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/558
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":558,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":15,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Turned out at &frac12; past 6 o'clock. Found that J Galbraith had kept his watch last night for the first time. Have had a fair breeze all night from the SW. Continuing our course NE with steering sail set at the rate of 6 knots. Got fishing line out but could not catch nothing. The ship going too fast through the water.</p>\r\n<p>Bob &amp; I employed ourselves in painting the skiff &amp; making her decent to go to shore in at <span class=highlight_yellow>Elsinecra</span>.</p>\r\n<p>At 12 o'clock the Captain took his observation lat 55.1 long: 4.15 having been 112 miles since 12<sup>th</sup>  yesterday &amp; 123 from our departure.</p>\r\n<p>After dinner Bob &amp; I took a single glass of grog &amp; again set to work to finish painting the skiff. In the evening the wind changed to South. Got trysail set &amp; main tack down. At 9 o'clock had a glass of brandy &amp; water &amp; turned in</p>","pageId":739,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":536,"lock":null}
20:13:22.075 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
