20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":575,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<figure>\r\n  <img src=\"images/img2892-image-baltic.png\" width=\"600px\" />\r\n  <figcaption>Voyage for Copenhagen to St Petersburg</figcaption>\r\n</figure>","pageId":null,"type":null,"imageId":null,"marqueeId":null,"lock":null}
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/576
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":576,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning at a little after 7 o'clock. Bob &amp; I had some difficulty in making our minds up to turn out, the morning being so tremendously wet. The rain coming down in torrents with heavy thunder &amp; lightning, one peal so close to us that it sounded as if it was our top gallant mast head. In fact we were enveloped in clouds, thunder &amp; lightning, much too near to be agreeable. This deluge of rain has continued since 4 o'clock. However the wind was fair for us, a fresh breeze at SW &amp; giving 6 knots, our course NE by E. At 9 o'clock the rain eased &amp; by 10 o'clock we had fine weather, warm wind the <span class=highlight_yellow>lamed</span> &amp; going 5 &frac12; knots, with studding sails set. At 12 o'clock we were by observance in lat: 58.3 long: 20.50. Having come 110 miles since yesterday at noon. Little or nothing has ...</p>","pageId":744,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":552,"lock":null}
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/576
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":576,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":2.0000,"text":"<p>Got up this morning at a little after 7 o'clock. Bob &amp; I had some difficulty in making our minds up to turn out, the morning being so tremendously wet. The rain coming down in torrents with heavy thunder &amp; lightning, one peal so close to us that it sounded as if it was our top gallant mast head. In fact we were enveloped in clouds, thunder &amp; lightning, much too near to be agreeable. This deluge of rain has continued since 4 o'clock. However the wind was fair for us, a fresh breeze at SW &amp; giving 6 knots, our course NE by E. At 9 o'clock the rain eased &amp; by 10 o'clock we had fine weather, warm wind the <span class=highlight_yellow>lamed</span> &amp; going 5 &frac12; knots, with studding sails set. At 12 o'clock we were by observance in lat: 58.3 long: 20.50. Having come 110 miles since yesterday at noon. Little or nothing has ...</p>","pageId":744,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":552,"lock":null}
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/577
20:13:22.084 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":577,"version":2,"year":1829,"month":6,"day":26,"sequence":3.0000,"text":"<p>... occurred today worthy of note. Since the morning we have had most delightful weather, with a few refreshing breeze at SW &amp; SW by W. Our course NE &frac12; E &amp; going 4 7 5 knots. At 9 o'clock Bob &amp; I finished two glasses of gin &amp; water &amp; at 10 o'clock turned in.</p>","pageId":745,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":553,"lock":null}
