20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":625,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":23,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Still wind bound, blowing fresh from the SW. Suffered a great deal of pain during the night from my tooth &amp; my face swollen to a giant size. Could with difficulty eat. In no ...</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":589,"lock":null}
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/626
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":626,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Fine weather but still a fresh westerly breeze, which detains us. Relieved from pain by the flannel &amp; hartshorn, but my face still much swollen. Remained on board &amp; upon the mole head all the morning.</p>\r\n<p>Mr Bookers clerk called to demand 15 rubbles brokerage for the copper. Went on shore at 5 o'clock to see him upon the subject. It appears I treated with a broker &amp; nob the merchant or I supposed, the brokerage therefore due. Desired Mr Booker to request Mr Hubbardth to pay it &amp; I should settle it in London, having no Russian money left. Amused ourselves on the mole head all evening</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":590,"lock":null}
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           Tree: null
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - publish: topic: diaries/fragments/626
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -          value: {"id":626,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":24,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Fine weather but still a fresh westerly breeze, which detains us. Relieved from pain by the flannel &amp; hartshorn, but my face still much swollen. Remained on board &amp; upon the mole head all the morning.</p>\r\n<p>Mr Bookers clerk called to demand 15 rubbles brokerage for the copper. Went on shore at 5 o'clock to see him upon the subject. It appears I treated with a broker &amp; nob the merchant or I supposed, the brokerage therefore due. Desired Mr Booker to request Mr Hubbardth to pay it &amp; I should settle it in London, having no Russian money left. Amused ourselves on the mole head all evening</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":590,"lock":null}
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise - Difference detected on topic: diaries/fragments/627
20:13:22.110 [main] INFO  com.rsmaxwell.diaries.responder.sync.Synchronise -           DB:   {"id":627,"version":0,"year":1829,"month":7,"day":25,"sequence":1.0000,"text":"<p>Blowing very hard during the whole of the day from the westward. Several ships arrived having had fine passages of 13 days from London. Amongst them the Sceptre, <span class=highlight_yellow>Coui</span> Greenhod. Several laden ships having hauled out made the <span class=highlight_yellow>roadshad</span> very <span class=highlight_yellow>throng</span>. On the mole head all the morning. Mr Lendaley came down in the steam boat &amp; brought <span class=highlight_yellow>me </span><span class=highlight_yellow>ec</span> ...</p>","pageId":760,"type":"MARQUEE","imageId":null,"marqueeId":591,"lock":null}
